}\uc1\pard\ul\f0\fs24 1.00 Introduction\ulnone\par
\par
\f1 P?S/8 supports a general-purpose assembler for \ul\i PDP-8 Assembly Language\ulnone\i0  (\i PAL\i0 ) known as \i P?S/8\i0  \i\f2 PAL\i0\f0 .  This document describes all technical aspects of P?S/8 PAL in terms of both language features and constructive usage; this includes certain tips and techniques for\par
PAL programming helpful to all users regardless of assembly language skill level.\par
\par
Note: The current release of P?S/8 PAL is Version 8S which is provided as part of P?S/8 Version 8Z; this includes a small number of documented binary patches made since the original 06-Oct-1987 release.  Specific patch details can be found within the \ul\i P?S/8 System Change Log\ulnone\i0  maintained as a separate document.\par
\par
LINC mode assembly (as used on LINC, LINC-8 and PDP-12 computers) is enabled by the use of certain command-line options; the implementation is generally compatible with the embedded assembler utility of the \ul\i LAP6-DIAL\ulnone\i0 /\ul\i DIAL-MS\ulnone\i0  operating system for the PDP-12.  When assembling LINC code (possibly interspersed with PDP-8 code), P?S/8 PAL can be considered to be a cross-assembler.\par
\par
While execution of binary LINC mode code requires LINC computer hardware, P?S/8 PAL does not require any additional hardware beyond the basic P?S/8 system for any PDP-8 (or DECmate) model to assemble LINC source code into binary code.  Depending on the specifics of the application, it may be necessary to devise utilities to write the developed program on the intended hardware.  (The LINC is not software compatible with the PDP-8; however, it is possible to write arbitrary data on LINCtape blocks formatted for use on the LINC.  No version of P?S/8 (or \i OS/8\i0 ) directly supports this media; as such, utilities must be written to transfer data as required to write data for the LINC.\par
There are similar requirements for use with analogous systems that run on the LINC-8 and the PDP-12.)\par
\par
Note: A list of supported P?S/8 hardware configurations can be found within the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  for P?S/8 Version 8Z (or newer); P?S/8 PAL is a standard component of all systems.\par
\par
For more information concerning dual-mode PDP-8 and LINC assembly, see Sections 2.10 and 2.11 below which document dual-mode assembly considerations.  As stated above: Depending on the ultimate target system for the binary output of the assembly, additional utilities may be necessary to load the binary data onto the intended environment.\par
\par
Unlike competing assemblers (\i TOPS-10\i0  \i PAL10\i0  and OS/8 \i PAL8\i0 ), there are numerous command-line option switches supported by P?S/8 PAL; this is necessary to allow the modular sections of P?S/8 PAL to be invoked as required.  Various portions of this document will explain these command-line switches including information on how best to deploy them for any given assembly situation.  Only certain features of P?S/8 PAL are needed in most typical assembly scenarios; as such, it is recommended that only relevant command-line option switches be set during any particular assembly operation.\page Note: \f1 Official releases of TOPS-10 PAL10 are of limited use for serious PDP-8 program development as compared to either P?S/8 PAL or OS/8 PAL8.  This is due to a small number of important features that are either entirely lacking or are misimplemented; as such, PAL10 is unusable for a significant amount of PDP-8 program development.\par
\par
This is ironic since, at one point in time, most PDP-8 program development (outside of the \ul\i R-L Monitor System\ulnone\i0  and all but the earliest implementations of P?S/8) was performed on TOPS-10 systems to take advantage of the then-considered-rich feature set of PAL10 and the advanced system features of TOPS-10 for program developers using (nearly) any language.  Unfortunately, as new features were added to other assemblers, PAL10 maintenance programmers made little (if any) changes; thus, PAL10 failed to keep pace with the emerging needs of PDP-8 system programmers who used either PAL8 or P?S/8 PAL (or both) including some of the newer features required for proper program development.\par
\par
It is hoped that proficient PDP-10 developers can produce an updated PAL10 release generally compatible with at least the important features that distinguish P?S/8 PAL (and OS/8 PAL8) from the present PAL10 release.  The developers of P?S/8 will gladly discuss these issues (and recommend that P?S/8 PAL features beyond compatibility with PAL8 be included).\par
\par
An important feature of the PAL language is the availability of several forms of literal statements; sections 2.06 through 2.09 below cover various aspects of literal usage and recommended syntax.\par
\par
Fortunately, the current PAL10 literal implementation is largely compatible with PAL8 (which is in turn a subset of the P?S/8 PAL literal implementation).\par
\par
The primary difference between the P?S/8 PAL literal implementation and that of PAL10 (and PAL8) is the conditional (or \i dependent\i0 ) literal, a feature conceptually unique to P?S/8 PAL.  Appendix C below discusses numerous issues related to conditional literals.\par
\par
\ul\f0 1.10 P?S/8 keyboard monitor commands to invoke P?S/8 PAL\par
\par
\ulnone Note: Command element descriptions in this document follow the conventions used in the \ul P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone .\par
\par
Command-line options are passed within P?S/8 in the same manner in which OS/8 \ul\i Concise Command Language\ulnone\i0  (\i CCL\i0 ) commands are formed (with certain extension features added).\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 supports several command prompt variations.  The prompt used throughout this document is identical to TOPS-10 and OS/8 to facilitate comparisons between the PAL implementations in the various operating systems.  \cf1\highlight2 <SP>\cf0\highlight0 , \cf1\highlight2 ,\cf0\highlight0  and \cf1\highlight2 <HT>\cf0\highlight0  are freely allowed between command elements to enhance command readability and are not required.  For a discussion of all P?S/8 command prompt options, consult\par
P?S/8 \i SET\i0  command documentation available elsewhere.\page Typical command examples (in slightly simplified form) are structured as follows:\par
\par
.PAL \cf1\highlight2 output files\cf0\highlight0  < \cf1\highlight2 input files\cf0\highlight0  \cf1\highlight2 option switches\cf0\highlight0\par
\i\par
\i0 or\par
\par
.PAL \cf1\highlight2 input files\cf0\highlight0  > \cf1\highlight2 output files\cf0\highlight0\i  \cf1\highlight2\i0 option switches\cf0\highlight0   \par
\par
Note: As shown above, the P?S/8 keyboard monitor allows command formation in either file specification order; this allows the user to work in a familiar environment regardless of prior experience (with various other DEC operating systems or similar).  The relevant commands are generally similar to various \ul\i concise command\ulnone\i0  implementations found in other DEC operating systems.\par
\par
The option switches can be placed anywhere in the command line after the program name (in this instance \i\f2 PAL\i0\f0 ) and may be stated redundantly.\par
\par
For more information about P?S/8 command formation and command-line variations, consult the \ul P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone  available as a separate document.\par
\par
\ul 1.11 PAL input files\ulnone\par
\par
PAL program input data is generally the concatenated contents of a group of \ul\i Tiny File System\ulnone\i0  (\i TFS\i0 ) text files passed in a specified order in the input section of the command.  Each file contains 2048 12-bit words, which will tend to consist of up to 3/4 of this capacity as six-bit bytes in the P?S/8 ASCII text (subset) character set (which includes the \cf1\highlight2 <HT>\cf0\highlight0  character).  Typically this results in about 3000 characters per TFS file (depending on average line width and attendant line number requirements).  Effective line width is greatly influenced by the use of \cf1\highlight2 <HT>\cf0\highlight0  characters, which generally occupy several printed spaces, yet are stored as a single six-bit byte each.  The recommended PAL syntax encourages the free use of \cf1\highlight2 <HT>\cf0\highlight0  characters as required.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 TFS text files include line numbers which are ignored by P?S/8 PAL.  Outside of the edit buffer of the keyboard monitor (which can modify the text contents and line numbers), the line numbers are permanent (once saved as a TFS file).  Most P?S/8 system programs ignore line number content within input files; exceptions include several utilities specifically designed to display or otherwise interact with line numbers (present only in TFS text files and not in other files, such as extended-length text files).\par
\par
The maximum number of files that can be passed to any P?S/8 system program is 17.  Since several file slots may be needed for output files, the effective maximum number of input files tends to be somewhat smaller (typically 14 input files to allow three output files).\page Note: P?S/8 also supports extended-length text files, any one of which can typically contain considerably more text characters than the entirety of 17 TFS files.  For more information regarding extended-length file considerations, consult documentation of the P?S/8 \i OS8CON\i0  program (or other similar file conversion utility programs) available as separate documents.\par
\par
\ul 1.12 PAL output files\ulnone\par
\par
As stated above, all P?S/8 TFS files are fixed size (2048 12-bit words).  TFS binary files are created in the versatile \i Slurp\i0  binary loader format, which is significantly more efficient than binary files based on paper-tape frame images (such as the standard DEC \i BIN\i0  loader format).  A typical TFS binary file contains nearly 6/7 as much binary data as a block-format core-image of identical size, without implementing any form of internal block structure.\par
\par
Binary loaders for TFS Slurp format binary files are available on all standard P?S/8 configurations; a generic virtual Slurp format binary loader is implemented in all systems.  Most systems also support a device-specific Slurp format binary loader (which will be the default loader if available).  Command-line option switches control which loader will be used in specific situations initiated by P?S/8 PAL command variations (\ul\i assemble then load and go\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i assemble then create bitmap then load and go\ulnone\i0 ).\par
\par
At the end of the assembly, the count of created binary files is displayed (along with other statistics); this allows any excess passed output files to be repurposed as desired.\par
\par
Note: If insufficient output files are passed, a \cf1\highlight2 BO\cf0\highlight0  (Binary Overflow) error message will be displayed on the system console; the assembly process will be aborted.\par
\par
\ul 1.13 Command-line option switches\ulnone\par
\par
The command-line option switches are passed in a manner consistent with CCL as implemented in TOPS-10 (and OS/8).  Two variant forms are supported as follows:\par
\par
\tab 1) One or more individual option switches each preceded by \cf1\highlight2 /\cf0\highlight0  such\par
\tab    as:\par
\par
\tab    /M /N /L\par
\par
\tab 2) Grouped switches preceded by \cf1\highlight2 (\cf0\highlight0  such as:\par
\par
\tab    (MNL)\par
\par
\tab    Note: The trailing \cf1\highlight2 )\cf0\highlight0  is optional unless needed to separate the\par
\tab    option group from another command-line section.\page Both forms of passing command-line option switches can be used together in the same command; all command-line option switches may be given redundantly without error.\par
\par
\ul 1.14 Passed numerical value\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL can optionally chain to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0 , the binary file loading and binary paper-tape utility program, for the purpose of loading (or punching to binary paper-tape) the binary files created during the assembly.  If desired, the assembled program can be automatically started at an address expressed as =\i xxxx\i0  where \i xxxx\i0  is up to four octal digits in the range of 0000 through 7776; for programs starting in extended memory, the /1 through /7 switches provide the starting field.  See Sections 2.02 and 2.30 below for more information.\par
\par
The P?S/8 keyboard monitor internally passes the value 7777 when an explicit numerical value is not provided.  When binary file loading is in effect, P?S/8 BIN uses this value to effect program start at a system-specific location somewhere within the system kernel area (07600-07777); a safe halt instruction is loaded at the selected location.  Pressing \cf1\highlight2 Continue\cf0\highlight0  on the front panel will effectively loop in place preventing potential problems; the user is expected to constructively start the program manually (at a user-determined address) using the computer front panel switch register.\par
\par
Note: By use of the P?S/8 \i GET\i0  command (or adding the equivalent options to the P?S/8 PAL command line), a core-image of the program can be created at the end of the assembly process to allow further program development.  Debugging and related binary program options are described in a separate document.\line\line On systems lacking front panels (such as the PDP-8/m or PDP-8/a without the optional programmer's panel, or any DECmate model) the GET command should be used to allow program execution control from P?S/8 \i ODT\i0  or other utilities.\par
\par
\ul 2.00 Command-line option descriptions\ulnone\par
\par
The following sections of this document describe command-line options grouped by common function.  Command-line option lists and additional information are provided elsewhere in this document.  Appendix D below contains a list of all command-line options in sorted order.\par
\par
\ul 2.01 Output file options\ulnone\par
\par
If explicit binary output files are not specified in the command line, the following command-line option switches can be used:\par
\par
\tab /B The \ul\i %\ulnone\i0  file on the P?S/8 \ul system device bootup logical unit\ulnone\par
\tab    (see description of the /U command-line option switch below)\par
\tab    is used as the (first) binary output file.\page  \tab /D If the /B command-line option switch is also set, the \ul\i $\ulnone\i0  file\par
\tab    on the P?S/8 \ul system device bootup logical unit\ulnone  (see\par
\tab    description of the /U command-line option switch below) is\par
\tab    used as the second binary output file (if required).\par
\par
\tab /U Change the logical unit that applies (when the /B and/or /D\par
\tab    command-line option switches are used as described above),\par
 \tab    from the \ul system device bootup logical unit\ulnone  to the value\par
\tab    determined by the following calculation:\par
\par
\tab    (\ul System device bootup logical unit\ulnone  \cf1\highlight2 XOR\cf0\highlight0  1); this selects the\par
\tab    \i opposite\i0  logical unit of the primary pair of logical drive\par
\tab    units.\par
\par
\tab    Note: On certain P?S/8 systems the \ul system device bootup\par
\ulnone  \tab    \ul logical unit\ulnone  can be non-zero (perhaps logical drive 1 through\par
\tab    7); as such, this method will generally achieve a useful drive\par
\tab    pairing.\par
\par
Due to the physical proximity to the beginning of storage devices, specifying the \ul %\ulnone  and \ul $\ulnone  files can noticeably speed up various system commands.  Additionally, these files are not subject to the overhead of TFS directory functions on the same logical device unit; as such, many operations have significantly lower overhead when these files are used strategically.  On tape-based systems, throughput can be further improved by using a logical drive unit different from the system bootup drive.\par
\par
Note: The /B, /D and /U command-line option switches are provided to simplify the specification of output files, as most assemblies require one or two output files.  All explicit file specifications can designate an optional device unit; as such, any combination of files can state associated logical device units as necessary.  The use of these command-line option switches merely allows specifying commonly used output file combinations in a more succinct form; however, if used in BATCH files, the actual files used could be determined by the particular \ul system device bootup logical unit\ulnone  in effect.\par
\par
If more than two binary output files are required, or the created binary files should be written to permanently named TFS files, the\par
command line must include explicitly stated output files.\par
\par
Note: If the second binary output file is not required, the /D command-line option switch will be ignored (effectively reducing the command-line to have been given with only the /B command-line option switch set regarding output file selection); the report given at the end of the assembly indicates the total count of binary output files actually created.\par
\par
\ul 2.02 Chaining options\par
\par
\ulnone\tab Note: Only binary output files created during the assembly\par
\tab process are used during chaining operations; excess output files\par
\tab (if any) are deleted from the passed file list.\page\tab Automatic chaining options can be invoked to further process \tab binary files created during the assembly as follows:\par
\par
\tab\f2 /G\f0  \f2 The\f0  \f2 files created during the assembly are passed as\par
\f0\tab    \f2 input files to the\f0  \f2 P?S/8 BIN utility for loading\f0  and\par
 \tab    execution\f2  \f0 (\f2 or\f0  punching binary paper-tapes as described below\par
\tab    in Section 2.30); a\f2 dditional\f0  \f2 loading \f0 (or punching) \f2 option\f0 s\f2  may\par
\f0\tab    be applied as necessary.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The chain to P?S/8 BIN is prevented if there were any\par
\tab    assembly errors to avoid execution of unknown instructions.\par
\par
\tab    With regard to chaining to P?S/8 BIN for the purpose of\par
\tab    loading binary files, see Section 2.30 below for additional\par
\tab    details; binary chaining options include the /H, /I, /V and /Z\par
\tab    command-line option switches and the \f2 =\f0 xxxx\i  \i0 passed numerical\par
\tab    value (possibly in conjunction with the /1 through /7 command-\par
\tab    line option switches).\par
\par
\tab    Note: The /W command-line option switch must be clear to allow\par
\tab    binary loading (as opposed to punching binary paper tapes).\par
\par
\tab    With regard to chaining to P?S/8 BIN for the purpose of\par
\tab    punching binary paper-tape output, additional binary chaining\par
\tab    options include the /F and /R command-line option switches.\par
\tab    See Section 2.30 below for more information.\par
\par
  \tab    Note: The /W option switch must be set when punching binary\par
 \tab    paper-tape output (as opposed to binary file loading).\par
\par
\tab /M The files created during the assembly are passed as input\par
\tab    files to the P?S/8 \i MAP\i0  utility to create a bitmap of the\par
\tab    assembled program indicating actual memory usage.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The format of bitmap output created by P?S/8 MAP is\par
\tab    generally consistent with the output produced by PAL10 after\par
\tab    any FIELD directive is used (or at the end of assembly) and is\par
\tab    a superset of the output of OS/8 \i BITMAP\i0 .  The title format and\par
\tab    pagination specifics are the same as those used for the\par
\tab    assembler listing output (if any) and assembly statistics\par
\tab    pages (which in part depend on the usage of the /N option\par
\tab    switch as described in Section 2.05 below).\par
\par
\tab    It is possible to specify chaining options to P?S/8 MAP and\par
\tab    P?S/8 BIN in a single command.  The chain to P?S/8 MAP will\par
 \tab    always be performed first; the additional chain to P?S/8 BIN\par
\tab    will be prevented if errors occur during the assembly.\par
\par
\tab    Note: When the P?S/8 MAP utility is used without chaining, \tab\tab    certain command-line option switches can be used to limit\par
\tab    which memory fields are eligible for mapping; during the\par
\tab    chaining process this feature is inhibited.  As such, all\par
\tab    memory fields are eligible for bitmap output following the\par
\tab    assembly; the output is limited to actually processed fields.\page\tab    For more information about mapping field choices, consult\par
\tab    documentation of P?S/8 MAP available in a separate document.\par
\par
\ul 2.03 Pass control options\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /K Perform a two-pass assembly; the default is a single assembly\par
\tab    pass.  \par
\par
\tab    Note: The /K command-line option switch is not required if any\par
\tab    binary or listing options are specified, as these functions\par
\tab    intrinsically require two pass operation.\par
\par
\tab    Unlike PAL8 which always performs two (or three) passes, P?S/8\par
\tab    PAL can perform a useful single-pass assembly (which is\par
\tab    consistent with the paper-tape system assembly program known\par
\tab    as \ul\i PAL III\ulnone\i0 ) as follows:\par
\par
\tab    At the end of pass one, all symbols still not defined are\par
\tab    shown on the system console terminal in sorted order; the\par
\tab    value indicated is the address within the assembly where each\par
\tab    undefined symbol was first encountered.  This facilitates\par
\tab    early program development when it is likely that (many)\par
\tab    portions of the program have not yet been completed.\par
\tab    Alternatively, variables and temporaries may not yet be\par
\tab    defined (or perhaps are the object of typographical errors\par
\tab    which can cause problems when certain symbols have names\par
\tab    similar to that of other symbols); most problems of this\par
\tab    nature can be quickly resolved by referencing the relatively\par
\tab    short list of undefined symbols.\line\par
\tab    Contrast this functionality with that provided by PAL10 or\par
\tab    PAL8, neither of which support a mechanism to highlight\par
\tab    undefined symbols.  The only facility provided in these\par
\tab    assembly programs is to create a second (or third) pass\par
\tab    listing file of the entire assembly; searching through this\par
\tab    far more voluminous output can be tedious as there are usually\par
\tab    several references to each undefined symbol as well as many\par
\tab    lines of output unrelated to errors.\par
\par
\tab    Note: In perverse instances, large quantities of erroneous\par
\tab    output could be triggered by the failure to correctly define\par
 \tab    key symbols during early program development; these\par
\tab    circumstances could make the manual process of eliminating\par
 \tab    errors far worse due to cascading dependencies.\par
\par
\tab    As such, using PAL10 or PAL8 can easily become a time-\par
\tab    consuming process.  In such circumstances, it would be prudent\par
\tab    to limit the assembly to a single pass using P?S/8 PAL until\par
\tab    key problems are solved.  Using only the undefined symbol\par
\tab    report (available at the end of pass one of the P?S/8 PAL\par
\tab    assembly), most problematic issues can generally be\par
\tab    eliminated.\page\tab    Using a second pass listing provided by P?S/8 PAL would be as\par
\tab    frustrating to use as that obtained from PAL8; however, only\par
\tab    P?S/8 PAL provides the one pass method that is recommended for\par
\tab    use during the early stages of program development.\par
\par
\tab    See the description of the /L and /N command-line option\par
\tab    switches for the alternate (and unrelated) usage of the /K\par
\tab    command-line option switch.\par
\par
\f2 Second \f0 pass \f2 assembly considerations.\par
\par
\f0\tab A second assembly pass is performed if binary output files are \tab created and/or assembly listing output is produced.  During pass \tab two, any additional error messages will be displayed on the \tab prevailing output device (system console terminal or lineprinter)\par
\tab in a format similar to that of PAL10 and PAL8 (including the\par
\tab nearest symbol plus any numerical offset) to facilitate source\par
\tab code development; in certain instances additional information may\par
\tab be displayed.\par
\par
\tab Programmers may embellish the displayed output of the assembly \tab process when critical sections are being processed as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) P?S/8 PAL supports the ERROR directive; this causes a\par
 \tab    deliberate assembly error.  By using the ERROR directive, the\par
\tab    programmer can design internal safeguards to prevent logical\par
\tab    inconsistencies that might occur during program maintenance.\par
\par
\tab    The design of safeguarded code sections generally includes the\par
\tab    use of conditional assembly statements to calculate\par
\tab    problematic scenarios; as long as the specific requirements\par
\tab    are met, there will be no output associated with the use of\par
\tab    the ERROR directive.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The output of the ERROR directive is consistent with\par
\tab    that of all standard errors during each assembly pass and can\par
\tab    include the display of an optional evaluated argument.\par
\tab    Automatic binary loading by chaining to P?S/8 BIN will be \tab\tab    prevented if the ERROR directive was assembled.\par
\par
\tab b) P?S/8 PAL supports the PAUSE directive both as originally\par
\tab    implemented in PAL III (to ignore the rest of the contents of\par
\tab    the latest input file) and also as an extended feature which\par
\tab    can embellish displayed output during the assembly process.\par
\par
\tab    When the PAUSE directive is used with an argument, the\par
 \tab    argument is evaluated as a 12-bit unsigned integer.  Displayed\par
 \tab    output is consistent with the format of standard error\par
 \tab    messages; however, usage of the PAUSE directive does not\par
\tab    report as an error.\page\tab    The programmer can utilize the PAUSE directive for a variety\par
\tab    of purposes (such as announcing the assembly pass or start of\par
 \tab    assembly of a critical section).  When a logical inconsistency\par
\tab    is detected, the PAUSE directive can be used to output\par
\tab    additional information about the error to assist maintenance\par
\tab    programmers to obtain an understanding of the nature of the\par
\tab    problem.\par
\par
\tab    The techniques described above and other features unique to\par
 \tab    P?S/8 PAL can be qualified to prevent use with incompatible\par
 \tab    assemblers.\par
\par
\tab    The \i PQS\i0  directive is defined only in P?S/8 PAL; this allows\par
 \tab    the programmer to create conditional assembly statements to\par
 \tab    restrict usage of these features to P?S/8 PAL and not generate\par
\tab    erroneous errors caused by the failure to support the features\par
\tab    in other assemblers.  An example of this usage is as follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab IFNDEF\tab PQS\tab <PQS=  0>\tab /ZERO ONLY IF NOT P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab IFNZRO\tab PQS\tab <PAUSE .>\tab /DISPLAY CURRENT ORIGIN.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The XLIST directive, as implemented in P?S/8 PAL, is\par
 \tab    consistent with PAL10 and PAL8; XLIST statements can be used\par
\tab    to prevent output of portions of an assembly as necessary,\par
\tab    such as assembler-specific statements as described above.\par
\tab    When deployed judiciously, all unassembled conditional\par
\tab    assembly can be hidden from view when producing a second-pass\par
\tab    listing; the only indication of this technique will be a\par
\tab    corresponding change in the statement number field when normal\par
\tab    listing output is restored.\par
\par
\ul 2.04 Symbol table options\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /S The symbol table will be output at the end of the assembly.\par
\par
\tab    If the /N command-line option switch is also set, the symbol\par
\tab    table format will be consistent with that of an enhanced\par
\tab    listing pass (if enabled).\par
\par
\tab    Note: The exact output may be subject to the number of\par
 \tab    assembly passes performed; two pass assembly is used if\par
 \tab    binary output files are created and/or listing output is \tab\tab    produced, or the /K option switch is set.\par
\par
\tab /A If the /S command-line option switch is set, the entire symbol\par
\tab    table will be output at the end of the assembly; this includes\par
\tab    all permanent symbols including assembly directives.\par
\par
\tab    Note: If dual-mode (PDP-8 and LINC) assembly is in effect, the\par
\tab    symbol table will be set to that which applies at the end of\par
\tab    the assembly; certain symbols with dual definitions will\par
\tab    display values consistent with the final assembly mode.\page\tab    Certain permanent symbols may have been redefined during the\par
\tab    assembly by source code statements; this is especially\par
\tab    important in LINC mode assembly where there may be multiple\par
\tab    definitions of certain instructions that are model-dependent\par
\tab    within the various LINC hardware implementations.\par
\par
\tab    While all default LINC mode definitions within P?S/8 PAL are\par
\tab    for the PDP-12 LINC variant, source code changes can be made\par
\tab    to redefine affected symbols for compatibility with the LINC-8\par
\tab    or classic LINC as required.  Permanent change of certain \i SKP\par
\i0\tab    class (or other LINC instructions) can only be made at the\par
\tab    source-code level of P?S/8 PAL itself; as such, it is\par
\tab    recommended that all affected symbols be redefined in user\par
 \tab    source code statements as required.\par
\par
\ul 2.05 Listing options\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /L Assembly listing output will be produced.\par
\par
\tab    Unlike PAL10 and PAL8, P?S/8 PAL performs (at most) two\par
\tab    passes.  P?S/8 PAL is capable of simultaneously creating\par
\tab    binary file and listing output during pass two; as such, the\par
\tab    following situation can be avoided:\par
\par
\tab    Obscure PAL language quirks can be problematic when literals\par
\tab    are in effect.  Novice programmers must avoid certain\par
\tab    scenarios where the definition of certain symbols and values\par
\tab    is dependent on the number of passes performed as well as\par
\tab    specific addresses of generated literals.\par
\par
\tab    While dependence on specific literal addresses is very poor\par
 \tab    programming technique (and usually avoided by experienced\par
 \tab    programmers), it can be confusing to debug mystifying results\par
\tab    obtained from PAL8; PAL8 binary output is created using\par
\tab    symbolic definitions obtained at the end of pass one, possibly\par
\tab    updated during pass two.  However, PAL8 listing output may be\par
\tab    created using symbolic definitions obtained at the end of pass\par
\tab    two and possibly updated during pass three.\par
\par
\tab    Unless the programmer is careful to avoid pass-dependent\par
\tab    definitions, the binary output may differ from the values\par
\tab    indicated in the listing output (which may appear correct).\par
\tab    Since P?S/8 PAL always creates all output during pass two,\par
\tab    there cannot be pass-dependent differences between binary\par
\tab    output and listing output.  Relevant programming blunders\par
\tab    should be discovered sooner using P?S/8 PAL (along with\par
\tab    additional experience when the improper techniques are\par
\tab    recognized and avoided).\page\tab /P Enable wide-printing listing output.  Note: This option\par
\tab    primarily affects symbol table output allowing additional\par
\tab    columns of printed symbols per page.  (The expected output\par
\tab    format changes from 72/80 columns to 132 columns as often\par
\tab    implemented on lineprinters that use 11" x 17" ledger/tabloid\par
\tab    paper stock.)\par
\par
\tab    Note: PAL8 only creates output for a minimal line width to\par
\tab    (nearly) guarantee compatibility with narrow-carriage devices\par
\tab    such as the LP01 lineprinter, despite the fact that most\par
\tab    printers support 132 column mode; outputting to an LP01 may\par
\tab    cause excessive truncation.\par
\par
\tab    P?S/8 PAL supports source statements significantly wider than\par
\tab    PAL8.  Where relevant, the listing output of P?S/8 PAL will\par
\tab    avoid truncation (unlike PAL8) when assembling the identical\par
\tab    source code.\par
\par
\tab    Note: PAL8 users must exercise caution when creating\par
\tab    (somewhat) wide program statements.  Due to quirks in the PAL8\par
\tab    implementation, the precise number of characters leading to\par
\tab    truncation problems varies with statement type and other\par
\tab    unexpected issues.\par
\par
\tab    Depending on specific circumstances, a portion of the intended\par
\tab    source statement may be truncated.  This can unexpectedly\par
\tab    change logical program flow.\par
\par
\tab    Warning: This is not merely a matter of truncating the\par
\tab    listing output; rather, part of the source code statement may\par
\tab    be ignored.  Debugging unexpectedly truncated assembly\par
\tab    statements may be difficult (especially when conditional\par
\tab    assembly techniques are used in conjunction with the XLIST\par
\tab    directive).\par
\par
\tab /T Prevent output to the system lineprinter.  All output is\par
\tab    directed to the system console terminal.  See the description\par
\tab    of the /N command-line option switch below.\par
\par
\tab /N Output \i niceties\i0  are applied to the listing output including\par
\tab    titles on each printed page.  If output is directed to the\par
\tab    system console (instead of the system lineprinter), the output\par
\tab    will produce alternatives to form-feeds by the use of tear-off\par
\tab    lines instead of \highlight3 <FF>\highlight0  characters; this will ensure complete\par
   \tab    compatibility with system console devices that use roll paper\par
\tab    such as the Teletype Model 33.\par
\par
\tab    Note: For systems lacking a lineprinter that support hard copy\par
\tab    console terminals such as the LA36 (which can properly support\par
\tab    \highlight3 <FF>\highlight0  characters), the \ul\i Logical Console Overlay\ulnone\i0  can be\par
\tab    configured to redirect lineprinter output to the same physical\par
\tab    device as that used for console output.  For more information,\par
\tab    consult documentation of the P?S/8 Logical Console Overlay\par
\tab    available separately.\page\tab    When the /N command-line option switch is set, one additional\par
\tab    page of memory is allocated.  See Section 2.13 below regarding\par
 \tab    memory management options for more information.\par
\par
\tab /K Enable assembly title text from each input file.\par
\par
\tab    When the /L command-line option switch is used in conjunction\par
\tab    with the /N command-line option switch, the title field text\par
\tab    for the entire assembly is taken from the first line of the\par
\tab    first input file.\par
\par
\tab    Typically, assemblies are processed by concatenating the\par
\tab    contents of multiple TFS text files.  In most cases there is\par
\tab    no particular connection between program sections in different\par
\tab    files and intended assembly title changes; file boundaries are\par
\tab    merely an independent file contents consideration.\par
\par
\tab    To maintain compatibility with PAL10 and PAL8, taking the\par
\tab    title field characters from the first line of every input file\par
\tab    can be enabled; the /K command-line option switch must be\par
\tab    given along with the /L and /N command-line option switches.\par
\par
\tab    As long as each logically independent section of a program is\par
\tab    contained within either an extended-length file (or a single\par
\tab    TFS file such as a small parameter setup file), meaningful\par
\tab    title changes can be properly managed.\par
\par
\tab    As a practical matter, intentional use of assembly title field\par
\tab    updates via file breaks is used infrequently.  For example,\par
\tab    the source code of \ul\i FOCAL, 1969\ulnone\i0  updates the title field only\par
\tab    once during the entire assembly; as such, two extended-length\par
\tab    text files (for the two main modules known as \i FOCAL\i0  and \i FLOAT\i0  \par
\tab    respectively) support the intended title field changes during\par
\tab    the assembly.\par
\par
\tab    Additionally, the title on the assembly can also be changed\par
\tab    using other methods that are independent of file boundaries.\par
\tab    See the description of the \i EJECT\i0  and \i TITLE\i0  directives in\par
\tab    Appendix B of this document for further information.\par
\par
\tab    Note: A two-pass assembly will occur because the /L command-\par
\tab    line option switch is set to create listing output.  As such,\par
\tab    the /K command-line option switch should only be used to\par
\tab    enable title field text obtained from the first line of every\par
\tab    input file.  The alternate usage of the /K command-line option\par
\tab    switch (to force a two-pass assembly) is moot in this usage.\par
\par
\tab /X Enable the creation of cross-reference output by loading the \par
\tab    cross-reference module; an output element is created for each\par
\tab    symbolic (and literal reference if any) as well as the\par
\tab    associated statement number.  Cross-reference data is created\par
\tab    in statement order during the assembly; at the end of the\par
\tab    assembly process the data is sorted and included as part of\par
\tab    the overall listing output.\page\tab    Enabling cross-reference output causes all listing output to\par
 \tab    be several columns wider due to the inclusion of the lowest\par
\tab    statement number on the line at the left margin.  (The PAL\par
\tab    language allows multiple statements on a line separated by \highlight3 ;\highlight0 .)\par
\tab    Statement numbers are unique in the range of 1 through 99999.\par
\par
\tab    Note: It is strongly recommended that cross-reference listing\par
\tab    output with statement numbering be printed on 132 column\par
\tab    lineprinters, especially if the /P command-line option switch\par
\tab    is also enabled.\par
\par
\tab    If the /A command-line option switch is enabled, all symbolic\par
\tab    references will generate cross-reference output; the default\par
\tab    action is to reference only user symbols (and literals, if\par
\tab    any).\par
\par
\tab    When the /X command-line option switch is enabled, two\par
\tab    additional pages of memory are allocated.  See Section 2.13 of \par
\tab    this document regarding memory management options for more\par
\tab    information.\par
\par
\tab    Implementation Restrictions.\par
\par
\tab    Due to changes planned for release with P?S/8 Version 9A\par
\tab    (which is not yet available as of this writing), the cross-\par
\tab    reference output is currently written to the system device\par
\tab    logical unit 7 starting at block 0000; all prior contents of\par
\tab    this device will be overwritten as necessary .  No additional\par
\tab    output of cross-reference data is available; no final data\par
\tab    sorting or processing is provided in the current release.  The\par
\tab    cross-reference output is limited to the storage available on\par
\tab    logical device unit 7 (since only one device is used).\par
\par
\tab    Once the cross-reference output programming is upgraded to\par
\tab    allow allocation of extended-length files via a user-settable\par
\tab    configuration table, this restriction will be eliminated.\par
\tab    Significantly larger programs will be handled than presently\par
\tab    possible on appropriate configurations (such as RK8E/RK05-\tab\tab    based systems) due to the availability of multiple extended-\par
\tab    length files to store reference data during the assembly.\par
\par
\tab    As of this writing, the processing of the cross-reference\par
 \tab    output is not fully implemented pending the changes described\par
\tab    above with regard to cross-reference output data storage\par
\tab    allocation issues; however, both statement numbering and\par
\tab    creation of reference data are currently functional (although\par
\tab    the cross-reference information is not currently accessible).\par
\tab    Logical unit 7 must be available as described above when\par
\tab    enabling this feature in the current release.  (The\par
\tab    availability of logical Unit 7 will become optional once this\par
\tab    restriction is eliminated; extended-length files will be\par
\tab    configurable on all available logical drive units when new\par
\tab    options of the P?S/8 \i SET\i0  command are made available.)\page\tab    Additional required changes will be made to the System\par
\tab    Programs Directory (expected to be implemented in the next\par
\tab    full release) in conjunction with other performance\par
\tab    improvements.\par
\par
\tab    Note: PAL8 does not directly support cross-reference output.\par
\tab    As an alternative, OS/8 listing files are reparsed by OS/8\par
\tab    \i CREF\i0  (if enabled) as a post-processing utility.  There are\par
\tab    several disadvantages to this method:\par
\par
\tab a) The listing output of PAL8 is not a statement-by-statement \tab\tab    listing file; statements hidden by the use of the XLIST\par
\tab    directive (and related techniques) do not appear in the\par
\tab    listing file.  As such, the cross-referenced listing output\par
\tab    is potentially incomplete; statement numbers are inherently\par
\tab    inaccurate and untrustworthy.\par
\par
\tab    By way of contrast, P?S/8 PAL statement numbering is\par
\tab    compatible with PAL10, which correctly shows processed\par
\tab    statement numbers, revealing the presence of any hidden\par
\tab    statements.\par
\par
\tab b) A valid technique for including documentation within PAL\par
\tab    source code is to use deliberately failing conditional\par
\tab    assembly on a section of commentary as large as an entire\par
 \tab    printable page of the listing.  Content examples include\par
\tab    details regarding program operation and \ul\i banner\i0  \i pages\ulnone\i0  of large\par
\tab    character outlines created from a series of smaller\par
\tab    characters; the nature of this form of output is limited only\par
\tab    by the programmer's creativity.\par
\par
\tab    Unfortunately, CREF has no ability to discern this aspect of\par
\tab    a PAL8 listing file as commentary; as such, the commentary is\par
\tab    cross-referenced as if part of assembled code-generating\par
\tab    statements.  When banner pages are used, there can be\par
\tab    numerous erroneous junk references to symbols that merely\par
\tab    correspond to the individual characters used to build up the\par
\tab    larger character layouts; as such, the cross-reference output\par
\tab    can be cluttered with many superfluous references.  Since CREF\par
\tab    has limitations on overall symbol table size, this can have a\par
\tab    deleterious effect on the overall capability of CREF.  See c)\par
\tab    and d) below.\par
\par
\tab c) CREF internally reckons statement numbers as 13-bit integers\par
\tab    in the range of 1 through 8191 with an odd quirk: additional\par
\tab    statements wrap back to 4096 instead of 1.  As such, there\par
\tab    is inherent anomaly in the cross-reference output regarding\par
\tab    precise statement numbering of individual references.\par
\par
\tab    By way of contrast, P?S/8 PAL and PAL10 allow unique statement\par
\tab    numbering in the range of 1 through 99999.\page\tab d) CREF has limited overall capability.  If the entire cross-\par
\tab    reference cannot be accomplished in one pass, the program\par
\tab    aborts unless the /M command-line option switch is set.  If\par
\tab    the /M command-line option switch is enabled, CREF operates as\par
\tab    a rigidly-defined two-pass operation which may (eventually)\par
\tab    allow the process to complete.  However, each \i half\i0  of the\par
\tab    overall operation must complete to obtain a successful overall\par
\tab    result.\par
\par
\tab    Note: A quirk of the two-pass method used is the insertion of\par
\tab    an extraneous \highlight3 <FF>\highlight0  in the output stream when the second pass\par
 \tab    begins.  (This is documented but not explained.)\par
\par
\tab    The \i split-point\i0  for this so-called \i mammoth\i0  mode is set to the\par
\tab    theoretical symbol \i LGNNNN\i0 .  References to all symbols that\par
\tab    sort lower than this value are scanned during the first pass\par
\tab    while ignoring references to symbols that sort higher.  The\par
\tab    second pass processes all symbols that sort as LGNNNN or\par
\tab    higher, including all references to current-page and page zero\par
\tab    literals; the references processed in the first pass are\par
 \tab    ignored.  Each pass must scan the entire listing file without\par
\tab    symbol overflow.\par
\par
\tab    Because of the arbitrary nature of the split-point, it is \tab\tab    possible for the overall cross-reference to fail because the\par
 \tab    program symbols are not \i balanced\i0  sufficiently to conform to\par
\tab    this arbitrary scheme (for which there are no options).\par
\par
\tab e) Because of the inherently redundant nature of rescanning text\par
\tab    files attempting to obtain information about the assembly\par
\tab    without having access to the original symbol table and parsing\par
\tab    techniques, CREF operates very slowly.\par
\par
\tab    By way of contrast, assembler-based cross-reference\par
\tab    implementation has the advantage of only processing legitimate\par
\tab    references to symbols and literals; commentary is ignored.\par
\tab    Instead of rescanning entire listing files, the essential\par
\tab    cross-reference data is scanned as many times as necessary to\par
 \tab    process all references; there is no arbitrary static split-\par
\tab    point nor requirement of at most two passes on the far smaller\par
\tab    set of data.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Many large PAL programs cannot be fully processed by\par
\tab    CREF.  This includes (ironically) the source code of P?S/8\par
\tab    PAL (and many larger programs).\par
\par
\ul 2.06 Literal and link options\par
\par
\ulnone Properly utilized, PDP-8 literal statements allow rapid implementation of working programs.  That said, certain compromises might arise:\page A PDP-8 programmer may be concerned with producing the smallest possible coding for a specific program segment.  Certain portions of operating systems have stringent code space requirements with regard to an extremely limited program structure such as a device handler.  Often the expediency of using literals must be abandoned in favor of using a variety of tricks for which there is no analogue in other computer architectures:\line\line\tab a) Intrepid programmers can create equivalent literal values in\par
\tab    various ways.  The techniques generally use either the\par
\tab    contrived location of instructions to force generation of\par
\tab    needed constants or the use of seemingly superfluous OPR\par
\tab    statement conditions that can create fortuitous constants\par
\tab    regardless of code position.  In rare cases, multiple\par
\tab    contrivances are used in concert to achieve even smaller\par
\tab    binary code.  The ability to perform extreme optimization\par
  \tab    techniques requires intimate knowledge of the PDP-8\par
\tab    instruction set in terms of operation code values and bit wise\par
\tab    specifics.\line\line\tab b) When the contrived literal values are achieved, the use of\par
\tab    generated literal statements must generally be abandoned.\par
\tab    As such, this extreme form of optimization contraindicates\par
\tab    the convenience of using generated literals.\par
\par
\tab c) Purely for documentation purposes, some programmers use source\par
\tab    code statements that include the specifics of the equivalent\par
\tab    literal; however, that portion of the statement is part of an\par
\tab    extended comment.  This usage explains the intentions of the\par
\tab    programmer prior to the optimization.\par
\par
\tab d) In general, the use of literals discourages code space\par
\tab    optimization; as such, literals should be used if as and when\par
\tab    appropriate.  Many operating system components contain once-\tab\par
\tab    only initialization sections which are later repurposed as\par
\tab    buffers or table/list space.  It is totally appropriate to\par
\tab    include literal statements within such code; generally, there\par
\tab    is little to be gained using optimization techniques within\par
\tab    these memory areas.\par
\par
\tab e) Beginning programmers are encouraged to learn the proper use\par
\tab    of literals just after mastering the general instruction set.\par
\tab    Code optimization is not a factor until the programmer is\par
\tab    quite experienced.  As required, programs can be (partially)\par
\tab    optimized by removing literal statements as appropriate once\par
\tab    the complexity of the project clearly demands optimization\par
\tab    (after the basic structure has been realized).\par
\par
\f1 Since PDP-8 code is generally efficient for most applications, a common technique is the use of literals during early development, perhaps in conjunction with conditional literals as described below.  Only when necessary, optimization techniques can be applied (while simultaneously removing literal statements).\page Many of the system components of P?S/8 do not use literals while other sections use literals in at least one of several contexts usually related to program initialization.  This includes the implementation of P?S/8 PAL itself.  Most conforming user-written programs use identical techniques during development.\par
\par
In P?S/8 PAL, literals and/or generated links are enabled using a combination of command-line option switches as follows:\par
\par
\f0\tab /Q Enable generation of current page and page zero literals.\par
 \tab    Link generation is disabled; attempts at off-page references\par
\tab    will be treated as errors.\par
\par
\tab /O Enable generation of current page and page zero literals.\par
\tab    Link generation is enabled; however, links will be flagged \tab\tab    as errors.\par
\par
\tab /E Reset the current page literal extent when leaving the latest\par
\tab    page.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The use of the /E \f1 command-line \f0 option switch requires\par
\tab    some combination of the /Q and /O \f1 command-line \f0 option switches\par
\tab    to be meaningful.\par
\par
Enabling literal generation and/or link generation requires three additional pages of allocated memory.  See Section 2.13 of this document for more information regarding memory allocation and related\par
issues.\par
\par
Using the /Q \f1 command-line \f0 option switch in conjunction with the /O \f1 command-line \f0 option switch will suppress error messages on generated links; however, the count of links generated is always included in the statistics page produced at the end of the assembly.\par
\par
Assembly listing lines containing generated links will be flagged with a \highlight3\f2 '\highlight0\f0  character placed to the right of the generated instruction value. This feature cannot be disabled in P?S/8 PAL, PAL8 or PAL10.\par
\par
Note: PAL8 and PAL10 are compatible with P?S/8 PAL with regard to all\par
aspects of the disposition of generated link statements; however, all features regarding literals in PAL8 cannot be disabled.  See section 2.07 below for detailed warnings regarding generated links that apply to all major PDP-8 assemblers.\par
\par
\ul 2.07 Limitations of generated links\ulnone\par
\par
In general, generated links should be completely avoided; the shortest version: \b Generated links do not work!\par
\par
\b0 While this simple caveat ought to be sufficient, a study of the PDP-8 architecture reveals specific problems as follows:\page 1) Link generation is an attempt to partially abstract and obfuscate\par
   the PDP-8 architecture.  Unfortunately, this leads to sloppy\par
   programming techniques that (might) function while violating the\par
   hard-wired rules of the PDP-8 instruction set.\par
\par
   A generated link is an indirect memory reference used to modify a\par
   malformed attempt at a direct memory reference that cannot be\par
   achieved; this is accomplished by using an address literal created\par
   in a manner analogous to that used within literal statements.\par
\par
   The program specifies a direct memory reference that is impossible\par
   due to the violation of memory addressing rules; instead an\par
   indirect reference is automatically created by the assembler\par
   (assuming this feature is enabled).  Since this process has much\par
   in common with the creation of literals, all other aspects of\par
   literal generation applies.\par
\par
   The following assembled code fragment illustrates an attempt to\par
   violate the rules of the PDP-8 instruction set; the flawed\par
   statement is flagged as an error because link generation was not\par
   enabled.\par
\par
          *0200          *200            /TYPICAL START ON PAGE 1.\par
\par
   IR  0577  \par
   000200  1377          TAD     577     /THIS REALLY CAN'T WORK\par
\par
   The attempted usage cannot be achieved since the operand is outside\par
   of the allowed address range (0200-0377 in this instance); location\par
   0577 is part of the following page (0400-0577).  For an example\par
   such as this, locations on any page from 2 (0400-0577) through 37\par
   (7600-7777) are equally inaccessible.  The instruction is flagged\par
   with the \highlight3 IR\highlight0  error message (Illegal Reference).\par
\par
   The next example is the identical code fragment assembled with link\par
   generation enabled.  No errors are indicated, but the statement is\par
   flagged with \highlight3 '\highlight0  indicating link generation.\par
\par
          *0200          *200            /TYPICAL START ON PAGE 1.\par
\par
   000200  1777'         TAD     577     /THIS REALLY CAN'T WORK.\par
          *0377 \par
   000377  0577\par
\par
   The generated code is an indirect instruction in the range of 1600-\par
   1777 as a consequence of the modification; (a direct instruction\par
   would be in the range of 1200-1377).  The code also creates an\par
   address constant; the table of literal/link addresses is dumped at\par
   the end of the assembly (unless instructions exist past this\par
   section to cause this page's literals to dump sooner).\page 2) If link generation is enabled, the same source code produces\par
   flagged output.  The instruction at 0200 is changed to \ul\i TAD I 377\ulnone\i0 .\par
   Additionally, location 0377 is generated containing 0577.  Ignoring\par
   for the moment the instruction is now an indirect reference to 0577\par
   using the pointer address 0377 as an intermediary, to a naive PDP-8\par
   programmer (unaware of the potential negative consequences), the\par
   intended logic is satisfied: the contents of location 0577 are\par
   added to the accumulator.\par
\par
   Note: In these coding examples, symbolic references are\par
   deliberately not used to avoid clouding the issues.  Actual\par
   attempts at link generation are usually expressed in symbolic form.\par
   Had the code been located in an accessible location, no link would\par
   be required.  Links often come about when code overflows the\par
   intended page into the next page.  By using purely numerical\par
   statements, the results are easily revealed.\par
\par
3) To the inexperienced programmer, the program fragment \ul\i works per se\ulnone\i0 .\par
   Unfortunately, in real PDP-8 programming, considerations outside of\par
   this example tend to crop up; programs that \ul\i had worked \ulnone\i0 suddenly \ul\i no\par
\ulnone\i0    \ul\i longer work\ulnone\i0  and might also cause unavoidable assembly errors of a\par
   kind that merely enabling link generation cannot remedy.\par
\par
   This programmer might be tempted to use a location already\par
   inaccessible (due to being located past the current page) as a\par
   pointer.  The assembler will appropriately mark this with the error\par
   message \highlight3 II\highlight0  (Illegal Indirect).  Since it takes a pointer to access\par
   indirectly the address of the pointer, it is not possible to use it\par
   indirectly.  Had the programmer obeyed PDP-8 instruction rules,\par
   this would have come about without unexpected surprise.\par
\par
   Before investigating the insidious nature of link generation\par
   failing to work when no error messages occur (just generated link\par
   flagging), it will be shown that \i certain\i0  forms of generated links\par
   actually work as intended, as long as the link generation mechanism\par
   is not pushed to the point of impossibility.\par
\par
   In all cases, links involving \ul\i Direct JMP\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i Direct JMS\ulnone\i0  instructions\par
   properly function in the sense described above.  (Statements\par
   attempting link generation of the form \ul\i JMS I\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i JMP I\ulnone\i0  cannot\par
   function as explained above.)  If this were the only issue, lazy\par
   programmers might have a valid excuse to enable link generation.\par
\par
   Unfortunately, all of the other forms of link generation may fail\par
   depending on the exact nature of the running program; the assembler\par
   cannot have knowledge of the programmer's intentions in the dynamic\par
   sense.\page    Repeating the generated link example from above:\par
\par
          *0200          *200            /TYPICAL START ON PAGE 1.\par
\par
   000200  1777'         TAD     577     /THIS REALLY CAN'T WORK.\par
          *0377 \par
   000377  0577\par
\par
   A possible use of the example program includes manual start from\par
   the computer front panel; this allows setting the data field (DF)\par
   to a non-zero value in the range of 1 through 7.\par
\par
   When started this way, the program does not accomplish the intended\par
   function; the contents of location 0577 in the data field are added\par
   to the accumulator.\par
\par
   In general, PDP-8 programs are written for the 32K memory address\par
   space.  The data field is often used as an internal convention of\par
   the program logic.  When the program uses direct references (as the\par
   nominal statements attempt to do in the example), the data field\par
   plays no role in the logic of the program.  Programmers who never\par
   enable link generation are certain their programs function as\par
   expected (as long as no other statements are flagged with errors).\par
\par
   Note: Lack of generated links is no guarantee there are no logic\par
   errors in the program; however, at least this area of bad design is\par
   avoided.\par
\par
4) As soon as extended memory factors creep into the logic of a\par
   program dependent on link generation, insidious failures start to\par
   occur.  The programmer who never learned the discipline required to\par
   avoid this programming pitfall is now faced with a nearly insoluble\par
   dilemma.  The only remedy to this situation is to \ul\i bite the bullet\par
\ulnone\i0    so to speak, eliminating all generated links as a first step\par
   towards debugging the program; by preventing the attendant error\par
   messages much of the problem will be eliminated.\par
\par
   Note: Cleaning up after link generation might involve a large\par
   expenditure of effort all at once; had links never been used, the\par
   work necessary to eliminate assembly errors might have been\par
   accomplished in small increments.  (And perhaps in the proper time\par
   frame to remember the constraints on the code as intended; all\par
   programmers are incapable of remembering everything, some sooner\par
   than others.)\par
\par
In short, link generation correctly functions only if \ul\i Direct JMP\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i Direct JMS\ulnone\i0  instructions are involved.  Link generation fails to work if any form of \ul\i AND, TAD, ISZ or DCA\ulnone\i0  instructions are involved because the logic is at the mercy of the very real possibility the data field (sometimes) doesn't match the instruction field when the program executes.  (Or the prevailing data field might change in a future variation of the overall program.)\page Positing a stubborn and lazy PDP-8 programmer (the author has met people who fall into this category; their careers as PDP-8 programmers who use generated links are always short), one of those who insist on using link generation despite all the warnings; their excuses are invariably within the list as follows:\par
\par
   a) Use only \i JMP\i0  and \i JMS\i0  links.\par
\par
   b) Never use the cases that cannot work and count how many links\par
      are generated in the assembler statistics page every time to\par
      ensure nothing important has changed.\par
\par
      There are inherent logical fallacies in a) and b) above.\par
\par
   c) One can only assume a) is true; it is necessary to check the\par
      entire program to ensure this is the case.  Confirming this\par
      is far more work than merely avoiding link generation.\par
\par
   d) The count of links generated may be the same as in a previous\par
      assembly.  However, this is still no guarantee all of the\par
      generated links avoid the impossible cases; if the code is moved\par
      around sufficiently, a \i JMP\i0  or \i JMS\i0  link could drop out and be\par
      replaced with one of the impossible cases in terms of the\par
      assembler's generated links tally.  It is still necessary to\par
      carefully check the entire program as in c) above; the\par
      unfortunate need for additional work still applies.\par
\par
   e) In general, people this stubborn are hypocritical.  They will\par
      tend to use the potentially impossible form of generated links\par
      claiming certain knowledge of the data field throughout the\par
      entire program.  This makes them vulnerable to the various\par
      insidious problems described earlier.\par
\par
   f) When cross-field calling conventions are established in\par
      meaningful programs, it is likely belatedly understood just how\par
      problematic the burden of link generation actually is.  The work\par
      involved in elimination of the problems can be profound.\par
\par
   g) In the case of P?S/8 PAL, PAL10 and PAL8, additional option\par
      switches must be set to suppress error messages associated with\par
      link generation.  This is by design, as the developers of\par
      successful PDP-8 operating systems virtually never use link\par
      generation; they are trying to guide programmers to success in\par
      PDP-8 programming in the sense of leading by example.\par
\par
\ul 2.08 Literal statement examples\ulnone\f2\par
\par
\f0 P?S/8 PAL supports the two general forms of literal generation along with a unique third form that can shorten program development time:\page\tab a) Current page literals - The operand address is located on the\par
\tab    current page of the program; the address is assigned by the\par
\tab    assembler starting at relative location 177.  Additional\par
 \tab    literals are created in reverse order; a second current page\par
 \tab    literal is placed at relative location 176.\par
\par
\tab    The maximum number of current page literals allowed is 63\par
\tab    (decimal) in the relative range of 101-177.  When a statement\par
\tab    contains a current page literal, the current page literal\par
\tab    table is searched for a matching value.  If successful, the\par
\tab    existing literal address is used.  New values create\par
\tab    additional current page literals.  When either too many\par
\tab    current page literals are attempted or statements on the\par
\tab    current page overflow into the literal table, a \highlight3 PE\highlight0  (Page\par
\tab    Exceeded) error message is displayed.\par
\par
\tab    A typical current page literal usage follows:\par
\par
\tab    \tab    \tab    TAD\tab (3)\tab\tab /ADD 0003 TO THE ACCUMULATOR.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The trailing right parenthesis character is optional\par
\tab    unless nested expressions are used.\par
\par
\tab b) Page Zero literals - The operand address is located on Page\par
 \tab    Zero; the address is assigned by the assembler starting at\par
 \tab    location 0177.  Additional literals are created in reverse\par
\tab    order; a second Page Zero literal is placed at location 0176.\par
\par
\tab    The maximum number of Page Zero literals is 112 (decimal) in\par
\tab    the range of 0020-0177.  (Locations lower than 0020 are\par
\tab    disallowed due to the special auto-index characteristics of\par
\tab    locations 0010-0017 when indirectly referenced.)\par
\par
\tab    When a statement contains a Page Zero literal, the Page Zero\par
 \tab    literal table is searched for a matching value.  If\par
\tab    successful, the existing literal address is used.  New values\par
\tab    create additional Page Zero literals.  When either too many\par
\tab    Page Zero literals are attempted or statements on Page Zero\par
\tab    overflow into the literal table, a \highlight3 ZE\highlight0  (Page Zero Exceeded)\par
\tab    error message is displayed.\par
\par
\tab    A typical Page Zero literal usage follows:\par
\par
\tab    \tab    \tab    TAD\tab [3]\tab\tab /ADD 0003 TO THE ACCUMULATOR.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The trailing right square bracket character is optional\par
\tab    unless nested expressions are used.\page\tab c) Conditional literals - The operand address is either on the\par
 \tab    current page or on Page Zero depending upon specific\par
\tab    circumstances.  If a matching Page Zero literal value exists,\par
\tab    it will be used.  Failing a Page Zero match, If a matching\par
\tab    current page literal exists, it will be used.  Failing both a\par
\tab    Page Zero match and a current page match, a new current page\par
\tab    literal is created (subject to the possibility of a \highlight3 PE\highlight0  error\par
\tab    if the current page overflows).\par
\par
\tab    A typical conditional literal usage follows:\par
\par
\tab    \tab    \tab    TAD\tab #3\tab\tab /ADD 0003 TO THE ACCUMULATOR.\par
\par
\tab    The actual code generated is dependent on the literal pools in\par
\tab    effect at the point in the assembly the usage is encountered.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Since there is no corresponding closing character, it is\par
\tab    impossible to create nested conditional literal expressions.\par
\par
\tab    A common technique employed in well-developed programs is the\par
\tab    use of source code libraries of commonly used subroutines.\par
\tab    It is desirable to use Page Zero literals where appropriate.\par
\tab    However, Page Zero literals should not be used if there are\par
\tab    only singular references.  By using conditional literals, more\par
\tab    effective management of memory is achieved without the need to\par
\tab    hand-optimize the library source code statements containing\par
\tab    literals.\par
\par
\tab    Additional information about conditional literals and related\par
\tab    subjects can be found in Appendix C of this document.\par
\par
\tab    Note: This feature is not supported by PAL10 or PAL8.\par
\par
\ul 2.09 Literal extent issues\ulnone\par
\par
In general, all literals associated with any current page should be defined in proximate order to the code statements referencing them.  This facilitates locating any potential matches to avoid creating additional literals (if possible).\par
\par
Leaving the present page, the existing literals are dumped to finish the intent and requirements of all source code statements defined so far.\par
\par
If this page is ever revisited during the assembly, it is possible to define additional literals because the literal extent value formerly in effect was saved and will be restored.  However, since the actual former literals have been dumped, every new literal statements will only be able to cause a search for a match within the newly-created literal pool subset.\par
\par
Note: This feature, while enabled by default, is hardly used.  It is supported primarily to maintain compatibility with the literal generation features of PAL10 and PAL8.\page If the /E command-line option switch is used, the literal extent when leaving a current page is reset; no history of previous literals on any current page is retained.\par
\par
Note: The use of the /E command-line option switch does not impact page zero literals (which are completely reset upon either the end of the assembly or the use of the FIELD directive).  In certain PDP-8 documents, this is referred to as \i forgetting\i0  current page literals.\par
\par
Resetting literal extents is useful when defining overlays.  The overlay code must be created in the context of another section of the assembly which presumably will be replaced in memory by the overlay code; however, the overlay code must maintain its own current page literals as required.\par
\par
while there are many techniques associated with the deployment of overlays, the /E command-line option switch is always required to allow overlays to maintain their own current page literal pools within the overlay section.\par
\par
Note: Future implementations of P?S/8 PAL (or a new assembler for the P?S/8 \i SHELL\i0  based on P?S/8 PAL tentatively to be known as \i PALX\i0  or perhaps \i XPAL\i0 ) will likely invert the default condition regarding literal extent retention due to disuse of the current default; as a practical matter, sections of program code that revisit formerly used pages are virtually always part of an overlay structure.  There are no justifiable reasons to deliberately separate small program sections that load on the same code page.\par
\par
\ul 2.10 \f2 Dual \f0 a\f2 ssembly \f0 m\f2 ode \f0 o\f2 ptions\par
\par
\ulnone\f0 P?S/8 PAL is the only known PDP-8 assembler that fully supports LINC mode assembly including conditionals and one's complement arithmetic, literals, large assemblies and the full complement of PDP-8 assembler features (including conditional assembly features generally associated with the \ul LAP6-DIAL\ulnone /\ul DIAL-MS\ulnone  embedded assembler).\par
\par
Note: While \ul LAP6-DIAL\ulnone /\ul DIAL-MS\ulnone  only runs on the PDP-12 computer, P?S/8 PAL does not require LINC hardware to assemble LINC mode programs.  Additionally, the \ul LAP6-DIAL\ulnone /\ul DIAL-MS\ulnone  embedded assembler does not support literals and many other standard PDP-8 assembler features.\par
\par
Dual assembly is enabled in P?S/8 PAL as follows:\f2\par
\par
\f0\tab /8 Enable LINC mode assembly; the default operating mode is\par
\tab    PDP-8 mode (\i PMODE\i0 ).  While not strictly necessary, it is good\par
\tab    practice when enabling dual assembly to state the desired\par
\tab    initial operating mode; this is equivalent to using the PMODE\par
\tab    directive.  The current location counter is set to the usual\par
\tab    PDP-8 starting value of 0200.\page\tab /9 Enable LINC mode assembly; the default operating mode is LINC\par
\tab    mode (\i LMODE\i0 ).  While not strictly necessary, it is good\par
\tab    practice when enabling dual assembly mode to state the desired\par
\tab    initial operating mode; this is equivalent to using the LMODE\par
\tab    directive.  The current location counter is set to the usual\par
\tab    LINC starting value of 4020.\par
\par
\tab    Enabling the dual mode assembly module requires the allocation\par
\tab    of three additional pages of memory.  Consult Section 2.13 of\par
\tab    this document for more information regarding memory allocation\par
\tab    and related issues.\par
\par
\ul 2.11 Dual mode assembly tips\ulnone\par
\par
While it is good practice to start dual-mode coding with the appropriate mode directive, it is possible (using conditional assembly techniques) to determine the current assembly mode.  References to the current definition of the \i HLT\i0  instruction (which will be 7402 in PMODE and 0000 in LMODE) can determine whether LMODE or PMODE is in effect.\par
Critical programs can abort the assembly if it is determined the assembly process is not in keeping with source code requirements (which could include failure to enable LINC mode assembly).\par
\par
While the use of literals generally applies to PDP-8 code, certain language features can be adapted to LINC mode programming using appropriate addressing restrictions.  Advanced users can contrive viable cases (minimally) worth pursuing.\par
\par
In general, LINC programming does not require literals due to significantly different addressing modes.  Immediate mode addressing conceptually replaces literals because the immediate mode operand cannot be (efficiently) used in other instructions.  Immediate mode can specify the address of common variables; this can be likened to the use of literals.  If the addresses can be contrived to conform to PDP-8 page addressing, actual PDP-8 literals can be used obtaining the intended results.\par
\par
Note: Any form of arithmetic expression used to calculate LINC mode addresses is performed using one's complement arithmetic.\par
\par
All LINC instructions on the PDP-12 and the classic LINC are implemented in hardware.  On the LINC-8, certain instructions are trapped and emulated using a support program known as \i PROGOFOP\i0  (\i PROG\i0 ram \i OF\i0  \i OP\i0 eration).  PROGOFOP is required for 100% emulation support; however, short sections of LINC programming can be initiated by PDP-8 programming with interrupt-handling optional.\par
\par
Note: The PDP-12 also supports an alternate form of trap simulator to allow programs written for the classic LINC and/or LINC-8 to be run as intended, perhaps with alternate interpretation of the LINCtape class instructions.  For more information, consult DEC documentation of the PDP-12 trap simulator.\page\ul 2.12 LINCtape information\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 and OS/8 configured for PDP-12 TC12 LINCtape operation use 128 words/block LINCtapes; \ul LAP6-DIAL\ulnone /\ul DIAL-MS\ulnone  uses 256 words/block LINCtapes formatted significantly longer than the maximum length handled by the classic LINC (512 blocks maximum).  However, certain DIAL-MS configurations have limitations when accessing blocks larger than the classic LINC limit (0777 octal) such as when logical storage units are implemented on the RK8E/RK05.\par
\par
This is apparently caused by some internal confusion about the size of a logical unit sometimes allowed to be exceeded by hardware limitations; the RK05 is arbitrarily divided into logical units exactly the size of the classic LINC tape storage as opposed to the typical amount of storage available on the DIAL-MS extended-length LINCtapes.  Perhaps further information can be obtained from maintenance programmers, since this was a later add-on to this system and not part of the original system as created primarily by Jack Burness; the rest of DIAL-MS\i  \i0 predates the existence of the available hardware (the RK8F added to a DW8E-P).\par
 \par
Utilities to access 256 words/block LINCtapes require custom programming specific to each machine.  To transfer assembled programs to the classic LINC requires a custom image utility that runs under the current operating system; conventions for LINC operating systems vary widely, other than the same underlying LINCtape format.  LINC systems cannot access 128-word LINCtapes associated with P?S/8 and OS/8; using custom programming, the LINC-8 can handle all formats because the underlying hardware is actually a PDP-8 storage peripheral.  The PDP-12 TC12 LINCtape subsystem supports extended operations to work with any viable LINCtape format.  P?S/8 PAL can be instrumental in creating appropriate utilities as needed.\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL includes all common LINCtape instruction definitions as used on the LINC, LINC-8 and the PDP-12.  These are generally double-word instructions with block information in the second word immediately following the LINCtape instruction.\par
\par
Note: No useful purpose is served creating 129 words/block LINCtapes for use with P?S/8 or OS/8 on the PDP-12.  There are only downsides to doing so as follows:\line\line\tab a) The only reason 129 words/block LINCtapes exist is because the\par
\tab    most common LINCtape formatting programs on the PDP-12 were\par
\tab    created as part of the \ul LAP6-DIAL\ulnone /\ul DIAL-MS\ulnone  operating system.\par
\tab    Ignorant decisions were made to only provide 129 word format\par
\tab    (when choosing other than 256 words/block options).  There are\par
\tab    no circumstances where this option is ever constructively\par
\tab    used.  (Contrast this with LINC-8 MARK program (\i MARKL8\i0 )\par
\tab    options that generally include standard 256 words/block\par
\tab    LINCtapes and 128 words/block LINCtapes only.)\page\tab    Note: It is understandable how this blunder came about.  Only\par
 \tab    those familiar with PDP-8 DECtape software understand the\par
\tab    relevant reasons (and apparently the software staff of the\par
\tab    \ul LAP6-DIAL\ulnone /\ul DIAL-MS\ulnone  project were not aware of any of this and\par
\tab    likely guessing).  Only the Disk Monitor on DECtape actually\par
\tab    uses the 129th word on DECtape blocks for data purposes.  All\par
\tab    other supported systems use the first 128 words of each block.\par
\tab    Thus, the 129th word is ignored and merely present because all\par
\tab    DECtapes must be an integer multiple of 18-bit words for\par
\tab    compatibility issues that do not pertain to LINCtapes.\par
\par
\tab    The P?S/8 version of the \i MARK12\i0  formatting program is modified\par
\tab    to create 128 words/block LINCtapes as used on the LINC-8 and\par
\tab    PDP-12; this applies equally to both P?S/8 and OS/8 on both\par
\tab    hardware configurations.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The source code for the P?S/8 version of MARK12 (as well\par
\tab    as the companion \i TC12F\i0  program for DECtape and LINCtape\par
\tab    conversion) can be assembled with P?S/8 PAL using the /9\par
\tab    command-line option switch to enable dual-mode assembly\par
\tab    starting in LMODE.\par
\par
\tab b) Both P?S/8 and OS/8 for PDP-12 LINCtape support a nuisance\tab\tab    feature to allow reduced functionality on 129 words/block\par
\tab    LINCtapes.  When 129 words/block format is used, the memory\par
\tab    location following the read/write buffer can be corrupted\par
\tab    during a read operation.  As such, all relevant handlers for\par
\tab    both systems preserve the potentially-destroyed word, then\par
\tab    restore it after the read has completed.  This also means that\par
\tab    system device handlers for both operating systems must be able\par
\tab    to function while potentially the contents of location 07600\par
\tab    are at risk.  As such, there is a small chance that if the\par
\tab    computer is manually restarted at an improper moment in time,\par
\tab    the system device handler might become corrupted.\line\line\tab    The circumstances that arise for this potential problem are\par
\tab    actually quite common; any core-image program that loads into\par
\tab    07400-07577 is potentially at risk for destruction of the\par
\tab    system kernel that starts at 07600.\par
\par
\tab c) The LINC-8 system (and non-system) device handlers for P?S/8\par
\tab    and OS/8 cannot handle the 129 words/block format LINCtapes.\par
\tab    In fact, no software exists to read or write such tapes on the\par
\tab    LINC-8 presently.  (Specific conversion software would be\par
\tab    necessary to manage this situation which is totally avoidable\par
\tab    if the proper tape format is used.  For example, LINCtapes can\par
\tab    be formatted on the LINC-8 with 128 words/block and copies of\par
\tab    the LINCtapes intended to be read on the LINC-8 can be written\par
\tab    on the PDP-12.)\page\tab d) All tape transfers are degraded slightly by the presence of\par
\tab    the erroneous 129th word.  This is because all blocks of the\par
\tab    tapes are needlessly longer; extra time is needed to move the\par
\tab    tape past all blocks between the present tape block position\par
\tab    and the target block during any read or write transfer.\par
\par
\tab e) Since each block is needlessly longer, the maximum number of\par
\tab    blocks that can be utilized is needlessly foreshortened.\par
\tab    Several standard tape lengths are generally supported.  This\par
\tab    will tend to make certain tapes ineligible for extra-long\par
\tab    formatting considerations unless 128 words/block is used.\par
\par
\tab f) As a long-term consideration, there is needless increased\par
\tab    wear and tear on tape media and drive tape guides.\par
\par
\ul 2.13 Memory management options\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /C Enable internal reconfiguration of assembler resources.  This \tab    may allow certain assembly configurations on smaller machines\par
\tab    to be viable at the expense of overall performance.  See below\par
\tab    for additional configuration details.\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL is designed to run on 4K or larger machines; additional memory, if available, is used to allow a larger symbol table which is generally the limiting factor with regard to overall program size that can be successfully assembled.\par
\par
When P?S/8 PAL is first loaded, the passed command-line option switches are checked to determine the running configuration size with regard to modular assembler components retained once the assembly process begins.  Deselected modular sections are removed; retained modular options are then relocated as required to maximize the space available for symbol table and input/output buffers.\par
\par
Depending on options selected, it is possible that attempts to load the symbol table can overflow the assembler's capabilities; the selected configuration cannot function with standard allocation of input/output buffers.\par
\par
To achieve a viable configuration, the number of input/output buffers can be reduced; memory space is then made available for additional symbols.  The assembly process will take longer due to fewer pages allocated as buffers (but will more likely succeed while handling more symbols).\par
\par
To gauge the magnitude of the problem on 4K systems, the following is a list of the extra memory allocation requirements (over the minimum needed for an assembly without any modular sections retained):\par
\par
\tab a) Niceties related to the /N command-line option switch (fully\par
\tab    formatted headers on specific aspects of the listing output)\par
\tab    requires one additional page.\page\tab b) Literal and/or link generation requires three additional\par
\tab    pages.\par
\par
\tab c) Enabling dual mode (LINC) assembly requires three additional \tab\tab    pages.\par
\par
\tab d) Enabling cross-referenced listing output requires two \tab\tab\tab    additional pages.\par
\par
Each page reallocated for symbol table usage adds the ability to support 32 additional symbols; an assembly with no modular options retained can support 288 additional symbols over the included internal symbol table.\par
\par
If the /C command-line option switch is set, the input and output buffers are reduced to a minimum complement causing the assembly process to take considerably more time; however, sufficient memory space becomes available to support any combination of the modular program options as described above.\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL is designed to dynamically take advantage of additional memory if available.  On all systems with 8K, at least another 3K of memory is available for the symbol table; as such, all modular components can generally be retained without restriction for most assemblies.\par
\par
Note: Certain P?S/8 configurations reserve the highest 1K (quarter memory field) available for the system device handler extension.  For all systems that do not require the handler extension, the full additional 4K of memory can be made available to P?S/8 PAL (and other programs).\par
\par
When 7K (or more) memory is available, symbols are not stored in field 0.  The /C command-line option switch can be used to sacrifice performance while allowing additional symbols residing in field 0.\par
\par
The maximum number of symbols allowed is 4095 (including all permanent symbols and directives).  If 16K of memory is available for symbols (20K total memory or more), there is no point in enabling the /C option other than to perform timing tests to measure the resulting performance degradation; the exact configuration with regard to the modular options becomes moot for larger machines.\par
\par
Note: PAL8 requires 24K (or more) memory to support the maximum symbol table size of 4095 symbols due to certain design limitations of OS/8 that fractionalize every memory field.\page\ul 2.20 P?S/8 PAL assembler performance\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL performance was compared to PAL8 using a deliberately biased configuration (in favor of OS/8) (and a stopwatch for accuracy).  While P?S/8 PAL took twice as long to assemble a large file chosen at random from various OS/8 source programs under development at the time, the P?S/8 configuration was based on TC08/TU56 DECtape while the OS/8 configuration was based on the RK8E/RK05 removable cartridge disk (which is a much faster system at the hardware level).  Given the overhead of the underlying system devices, it is estimated that P?S/8 PAL can assemble large programs as much as four times as fast as OS/8 PAL8 on identical hardware configurations.\par
\par
Note: Performance of the P?S/8 BIN Slurp format binary loader was compared to OS/8 \i ABSLDR\i0  on the same configuration.  The DECtape-based P?S/8 Slurp binary loader successfully loaded binary files into memory \b twice as fast\b0  as OS/8 ABSLDR on the RK8E/RK05.  This seemingly counter-intuitive feat, when demonstrated to various DEC PDP-8 programming group employees, induced a state of cognitive dissonance. They could not comprehend the notion that a DECtape could somehow perform faster than an RK05.  (Those familiar with the internal design of both operating systems better understand this predictable result.)\par
\par
\ul 2.30\tab Additional binary chaining options\ulnone\par
\par
Various options of the P?S/8 BIN utility apply during a successful chain operation.  Assuming no assembly errors, chaining to P?S/8 BIN is generally used for the purpose of loading the binary output files created during the assembly.\par
\par
Note: See the description of the /W command-line option switch below for the alternate purpose of chaining to P?S/8 BIN for the purpose of punching binary paper-tapes.\par
\par
As explained above, chaining to P?S/8 BIN creates a list of binary input files derived from the list of output files created during the assembly; this list may have been obtained from explicitly passed output files, or as a result of some particular combined usage of the /B, /D and /U command-line option switches.  As necessary, the list may have been truncated if excess output files were specified.\par
\par
The following additional command-line options of P?S/8 BIN apply if set in the original P?S/8 PAL command line:\par
\par
\tab /G Chain to P?S/8 BIN for the purpose of either loading the\par
\tab    binary files created during the assembly or punching binary\par
 \tab    paper-tapes of the binary files (if the /W command-line option\par
\tab    switch is also set).  The chain is inhibited if there are\par
\tab    assembly errors.\par
\par
\tab /V Enable the virtual Slurp format binary file loader instead of\par
\tab    the hardware-specific Slurp format binary file loader (if\par
\tab    relevant).\page\tab    Where possible, P?S/8 BIN loads binary input files using an\par
 \tab    implementation of the Slurp format binary loader as originally\par
\tab    defined by Richard Lary for the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone .  The P?S/8\par
\tab    variant supports loading anywhere within the 32K PDP-8 memory\par
\tab    space (albeit with restrictions that apply to system-reserved\par
\tab    memory areas).\par
\par
\tab    Specifics of the P?S/8 system configuration will indicate\par
 \tab    whether a hardware-dependent Slurp format binary loader is\par
\tab    available; only certain configurations support a device-\par
\tab    specific Slurp format binary loader.\par
\par
\tab    On systems that do not include a device-specific Slurp\par
\tab    format binary loader, a virtual Slurp format\i  \i0 binary loader is\par
\tab    used that requires the presence of the system device handler.\par
 \tab    Other than certain performance issues and minor tradeoffs, the\par
