\ul 2.40\tab Text management options\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /J Prevent generation of a zero-fill word (0000) after text\par
\tab    strings with an even count of characters.  This applies to all\par
\tab    usage of the TEXT and SIXBIT directives.\par
\par
\ul 2.41 Text directive issues\ulnone\par
\par
The PAL language defines the TEXT directive which is used with a string argument.  The string consists of an indefinite number of printable characters preceded by a delimiter character which cannot be included in the generated text; the string ends with a second usage of the delimiter character.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 PAL does not require the trailing delimiter character unless there are multiple statements on the same line.\par
\par
Early implementations of the TEXT directive restricted the delimiter character to \highlight3 "\highlight0 , \highlight3 '\highlight0  and in some cases \highlight3 @\highlight0  (which is not supported by P?S/8 PAL and other assemblers).  The use of \highlight3 "\highlight0  or \highlight3 '\highlight0  as text delimiters is recommended for maximum compatibility with other PAL assembler implementations; P?S/8 PAL and PAL8 support an extension allowing any\par
printing character not contained within the text string to be used as\par
the delimiter character.\par
\par
All usage of the TEXT directive creates strings of six-bit ASCII text\par
characters packed two per 12-bit word.  If the count of characters in the string is an odd number, the last 12-bit word has 00 in the low-order six bits (which generally is used as a string delimiter).\par
\par
Since the most likely usage of the TEXT directive is to generate strings used with printing subroutines, the default action for string arguments with even character counts is to generate an additional\par
12-bit word of 0000 to ensure the presence of a string delimiter.\par
\par
TEXT directive usage is not limited to string creation as described\par
above; some alternate schemes use internal printing characters as delimiters or other control mechanisms.  Within the P?S/8 keyboard  monitor coding, explicit string length arguments are used in some instances; in other cases the string length is an implicit constant value.  The 00 word plays no role in any of these cases; as such, creation of an extra 12-bit word of 0000 can be undesirable.\page Note: Early PDP-8 programs such as \ul FOCAL, 1969\ulnone  were developed on systems incapable of modifying the output of the TEXT directive; as such, the source code includes statements to backup the assembler current location counter to allow the desired contents which differs from the automatically generated 0000 value.  Ironically, this form of source-level workaround is incompatible with the /J command-line option switch (which eliminated the problem several years after FOCAL was developed).\par
\par
As documented above, setting the /J command-line option switch prevents the creation of the extra 12-bit word of 0000 on even count text strings.  It is strongly recommended the disposition of the /J command-line option switch be documented at the beginning of all source code; in many cases, this may be the only departure from assembler default settings and could easily be overlooked.\par
\par
\ul 2.42 Other text-related issues\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL supports the SIXBIT directive which uses the same overall format as the TEXT directive.  The only difference is the internal six-bit code used is not the PDP-8 standard six-bit subset of the ASCII character set; instead, an alternate definition of six-bit text (as used on DEC 18-bit and 36-bit computers) applies.\par
\par
The basic difference between the two character sets is the highest of the six bits used is inverted.  Input processing of seven-bit ASCII characters to six-bit code is easier using the PDP-8 standard while output processing of six-bit code to seven-bit ASCII characters is easier using the alternative standard.  The PDP-8 standard was chosen largely because there are often multiple input routines while output is generally performed by a single output routine.  For most programmers, it is easier to observe TEXT-related six-bit code to relate to the corresponding original seven-bit ASCII characters.\par
\par
Note: PAL8 and PAL10 do not support the SIXBIT directive.\par
\par
The DEVICE and FILENA\{ME\} directives are supported by P?S/8 PAL and PAL8.  These are special-purpose text directives to create a four-character and eight-character text string respectively.  Early OS/8 programs required the use of the /J command-line option switch (or its equivalent in PAL8 which is the /F command-line option switch); while this allows creation of the intended string arguments, compatibility is not possible with the usual string printing applications, which require even-length string termination with a trailing word of 0000.  With the availability of the DEVICE and FILENA\{ME\} directives, the normal flexibility of text string creation choice is restored.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 supports the FILENA\{ME\} directive for compatibility purposes only.  Future P?S/8 systems will support the P?S/8 SHELL with an extended assembler based on P?S/8 PAL to be known as \i PALX\i0  (or perhaps \i XPAL\i0 ).  PALX (or XPAL) may support an option to extend the FILENA\{ME\} directive (or some functional equivalent) to support SHELL directory file name conventions which are as follows:\page\tab a) OS/8 file names have six significant six-bit text characters.\par
\tab    As such, all of the characters are upper-case alphabetic or\par
\tab    numeric characters.\par
\par
\tab    OS/8 file name extensions are two characters or less in the\par
\tab    same character set as the file name.\par
\par
\tab b) P?S/8 SHELL file names have twelve significant six-bit text\par
\tab    characters.  The file names support case retention (which is\par
\tab    not the same as case-sensitivity).  References to SHELL file\par
\tab    names can be made in any form of mixed case usage.\par
\par
\tab    P?S/8 SHELL file extensions are up to four characters\par
\tab    expressed in the same character set as the file name.\line\line The PALX (or XPAL) language will include upper/lower case support to allow fully formed P?S/8 SHELL file names.\par
\par
In the P?S/8 SHELL environment, the file names are packed into six words containing the six-bit upper-case representation of the file names; the case bits are stored separately for the name and the extension.\line\line As such, two additional directives will be required, tentatively proposed as the CASENA\{ME\} and the CASEEX\{TENSION\} directives for the file name and file extension portions respectively.  The CASENA\{ME\} directive will return a 12-bit value with the actual case of the corresponding name characters obtained from the last usage of the extended FILENA\{ME\} directive.  The CASEEX\{TENSION\} directive will return a four-bit value with the actual case of the corresponding extension character obtained from the last usage of the extended FILENA\{ME\} directive.  Since trailing characters in both the name and extension portions of an extended file name may not be present, the case bits will only be set on actual lower-case characters in either portion of the last FILENA\{ME\} directive usage.  (The high-order eight bits of the returned value of CASEEX\{TENSION\} will always be cleared.)\line\line In a case-retentive system (such as the P?S/8 SHELL), the case of each file name character is retained for optional use in printing utilities; actual search functions are performed in a caseless manner.  Other than the length of compare strings, this is consistent with the basic P?S/8 system and OS/8.\par
\par
Note: To maintain complete compatibility with OS/8, a separate\par
FILNAM\{E\} directive may be used to support the SHELL file system names; the FILENA\{ME\} directive will also be available as presently defined.\par
\par
Certain user-modified assemblers exist that support the \i TEXTZ\i0  directive which forces the extra 12-bit word of 0000 even if the /J command-line option switch (or equivalent) is in effect.  Future versions of P?S/8 PAL may support the TEXTZ directive and the \i SIXBTZ\i0  directive (using the alternate six-bit text code).\page Note: As of PAL8 Version B0 (the last version released by DEC within OS/278 Version 2), the TEXTZ directive is not supported.\par
\par
Most PAL assemblers (including P?S/8 PAL and PAL8) support the \highlight3 "\highlight0  operator in expressions.  The result is the seven-bit ASCII code for the next character with the mark parity bit set.\par
\par
A proposed \i ASCII\i0  directive would take a string argument much like the TEXT directive to create a string of seven-bit ASCII characters.  Setting the /J command-line option switch would prevent a trailing 12-bit word of 0000.\par
\par
An additional \i ASCIIZ\i0  directive could be implemented to force the creation of the trailing 12-bit word of 0000 regardless of the setting of the /J command-line option switch.\par
\par
Note: An additional issue is whether to have an option to prevent the mark parity bit within the text string as arithmetic operations are not possible on individual string characters.\par
\par
Even without any form of ASCII (or related) directive as stated above, it is possible to create seven-bit ASCII text strings (with any of the variations required) using the \highlight3 "\highlight0  operator.  When used alone, each character in the string will have the mark parity set; string delimiter characters can be added if desired, such as ending the string with a 12-bit word of 0000 or making the last character of the string negative (as a 12-bit signed integer).  For example, by adding an additional term in each expression, it is possible to strip off the mark parity bit as follows:\par
\par
HLOWRLD,"H&177;"E&177;"L&177;"L&177;"O&177;" &177\tab /HELLO WORLD MESSAGE\par
        "W&177;"O&177;"R&177;"L&177;"D&177!4000\tab /NEGATIVE ENDS TEXT.\par
\par
An unnamed additional text directive has been proposed to create packed seven-bit ASCII text strings following the \ul\i three for two\i0  \ulnone packing convention as used in OS/8 (and P?S/8 SHELL) text files.  Because of alignment restrictions, additional null eight-bit bytes will be required, regardless of the issue regarding the mark parity bit.  If the string is meant to be an image of an actual file section, there must also be a mechanism to append a trailing \highlight3 Control-Z\highlight0  character into the string.\par
\par
Note: The issue of a \ul three for two\ulnone  directive will likely be taken up in the PALX (or XPAL) assembler which will support upper-case/lower-case text input files.  A \i PARITY\i0  directive with appropriate argument support can be implemented to handle all mark parity bit (and perhaps the \highlight3 Control-Z\highlight0  character) issues raised above.\par
\par
\ul 2.50\tab Additional command-line option switches and issues\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /0 Automatically generate *200 after the use of the FIELD\par
\tab    directive.\page\tab    Note: Most programs have data or explicit origins after FIELD\par
\tab    settings.  By using an explicit origin setting, a known\par
\tab    serious bug of one of the released DEC BIN loaders (known as\par
\tab    the \ul\i self-starting\ulnone\i0  binary loader) can be avoided.\par
\par
\tab    The problem with this version of the DEC BIN loader (which was\par
\tab    never fixed) is that when a FIELD setting is encountered, it\par
\tab    is applied prematurely; the last data word from the previous\par
 \tab    address (presumably intended for use with the former field\par
 \tab    setting in effect) is erroneously loaded into the new field.\par
\tab    By ensuring the last data word is an origin setting, this \tab\tab    problem can be avoided.\par
\par
\tab    As a practical matter, most well-designed programs use\par
 \tab    specific origins after FIELD settings for appropriate\par
 \tab    purposes; as such, this option is generally irrelevant.\par
\par
\tab /Y The \highlight3 !\highlight0  operator is changed from inclusive OR (.IOR.) to shift\par
 \tab    left six bits.\par
\par
\tab    Without the /Y command-line option switch set, 22!44 generates\par
\tab    0066; with the /Y command-line option switch set, 22!44\par
\tab    generates 2244.\par
\par
\tab    The \highlight3 !\highlight0  operator (with the /Y command-line option switch set)\par
\tab    can be used to create short six-bit text expressions:\par
\par
\tab    A=\tab "A&77\par
\par
\tab    B=\tab "B&77\par
\par
\tab    A!B\par
\par
\tab    Each word created using the \highlight3 !\highlight0  operator in this manner produces\par
 \tab    the same result as a 12-bit component (two characters) of a\par
 \tab    text string.\par
\par
\tab    Note: It is not necessary to use the \highlight3 !\highlight0  operator to create an\par
 \tab    equivalent result.  The following syntax will create the\par
\tab    identical value:\par
\par
\tab    A^100+B\par
\par
\tab    More complex text operations can produce similar results\par
 \tab    derived directly from the actual seven-bit character values:\par
\par
\tab    "A&77^100+"B-300\par
\par
\tab    By the use of these alternatives, the standard definition of\par
 \tab    the \highlight3 !\highlight0  operator is available (and use of the /Y command-line\par
\tab    option switch can be avoided).\page\tab    Certain programs require the default \highlight3 !\highlight0  operator when used to\par
\tab    embellish combined OPR or IOT instructions:\par
\par
\tab    CLA!CLL!CML!RTL\tab\tab\tab /SET THE ACCUMULATOR TO 0002.\par
\par
\tab    DTSF!DTRB\tab\tab\tab\tab /SKIP ON DECTAPE DONE OR ERROR FLAG\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /SET; READ THE ERROR REGISTER.\par
\par
\tab    Programmer preference will determine whether to enable the\par
\tab    /Y command-line option switch.  As in the case of the /J\par
\tab    command-line option switch, if the /Y command-line option\par
\tab    switch needs to be set, this should be documented in the\par
\tab    beginning of the source code to ensure proper assembly.\par
\par
\ul 2.60\tab P?S/8 PAL directives\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL is fully compatible with all standard directives (also known as \i pseudo-operations \i0 sometimes abbreviated as \i pseudo-ops\i0 ) as implemented in PAL8.  Certain additional directives are supported that are compatible with other assemblers such as the \ul LAP6-DIAL\ulnone /\ul DIAL-MS\ulnone\i  \i0 embedded assembler or PAL10.  Specific directives are unique to P?S/8 PAL.  There are serious issues associated with the \i DTORG\i0  directive as discussed below.\par
\par
Note: A complete list of P?S/8 PAL directives is provided in Appendix B below.\par
\par
\ul 2.61 Issues regarding the DTORG directive\ulnone\par
\par
The OS/8 implementation of the DTORG directive is deliberately avoided in P?S/8 PAL.  A proposed solution to the underlying problem is presented below which will allow the DTORG directive to be useful to P?S/8 system generation in future releases of P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
\ul 2.62 PAL8 implementation of DTORG\ulnone\par
\par
As currently supported in PAL8, the DTORG directive is completely compatible with PAL10.  The DTORG directive is meant to create (128 words/block) absolute block references within the binary output to allow a special-purpose loader (a component of the DEC \i Typeset-8\i0  group\par
system programming software) to write Typeset-8 system blocks on DECtape (and perhaps other devices).\par
\par
The general function is to specify a numbered storage block on a TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape followed by the (up to) 128 words to be written to the specified block.\par
\par
The origin in memory is set prior to the DTORG directive; however, it is generally ignored because no form of standard loader (such as the DEC BIN paper-tape binary loader) is supported.  The origin is used to define the upper limit in memory of the (up to) 128 data words that follow; once an entire PDP-8 page has been defined (placing the current location counter on the next PDP-8 page), the intended block contents are finalized.\page Typeset-8 programs create a series of small code sections as such. Each is loaded by a special-purpose Typeset-8 generation utility which writes the data to the specified DECtape (or other device) logical block.  However, there are important issues:\par
\par
\tab a) Each section written to a targeted DECtape block is taken\par
\tab    from a lengthy punched binary paper-tape created at an OS/8\par
 \tab    development site; the tape invariably is the latest release\par
 \tab    of a major section of the Typeset operating system (or one\par
 \tab    of its utilities).  In-house DEC Typeset-8 machines generally\par
\tab    did not include high-speed readers; these systems invariably\par
\tab    had Teletype Model 35 ASR terminals to read in the binary\par
\tab    data.\par
\par
\tab b) There is no form of DEC BIN-compatible checksum protection\par
\tab    associated with the written data.  Since the binary paper-\par
\tab    tapes are generally quite lengthy, this is patently foolhardy.\par
\par
\tab    By the nature of binary paper-tape, it is expected that\par
\tab    reading in long paper-tapes will occasionally result in read\par
\tab    errors.  This is why the DEC BIN loader format includes the\par
\tab    standard checksum protection frames.\par
\par
  \tab    Note: The DEC RIM loader, which lacks a checksum mechanism, is\par
\tab    only used to load short binary programs such as the BIN\par
 \tab    loader, which in turn can load in significant binary program\par
\tab    data with the confidence provided by passing an overall\par
\tab    checksum test.  It is unreasonable to expect to use the RIM\par
\tab    loader for lengthy binary files; the analogy between RIM\par
\tab    binary paper-tape loading and Typeset-8 binary paper-tape\par
\tab    loading is troubling.\par
\par
\tab c) Despite being created by PAL8 in OS/8 standard binary format\par
\tab    (as a .BN file), the binary output violates the OS/8 binary\par
\tab    file standard; the output of a program containing instances\par
\tab    of the DTORG directive cannot be successfully loaded by OS/8\par
\tab    ABSLDR.  Attempts to read in the punched binary paper-tape\par
\tab    will always end in a checksum error, even if an error-free\par
\tab    read was performed.  This is because the implementation of the\par
\tab    DTORG directive usurps the underlying DEC BIN format that OS/8\par
\tab    converts into \ul\i three-for-two\ulnone\i0  format OS/8 records (384 frames\par
\tab    per logical record).\par
\par
\tab    As such, it is impossible to improve the performance of the\par
 \tab    Typeset-8 special-purpose loader; reading in the entire binary\par
\tab    paper-tape will not yield valid checksum information that\par
\tab    could be used to reject attempts to read the lengthy tapes\par
        that introduce additional flaws.\page\tab    Note: It is thought that an alternate checksum scheme can be\par
 \tab    applied to the present incompatible binary format as described\par
\tab    here.  Compatible binary paper-tapes do not include FIELD\par
\tab    setting data in the checksum while the first frame of the\par
\tab    DTORG block argument sets the analogous bits.  However, it is\par
\tab    generally understood the Typeset-8 group never programmed for \tab    extended memory; it would appear that an alternate checksum\par
\tab    scheme is impossible if both DTORG and FIELD statements appear\par
\tab    in the same binary file.  It is not known if the Typeset-8\par
\tab    group programmers even included checksum tests; reading in\par
\tab    long binary paper-tapes and expecting the event to be free of\par
\tab    read errors represents a serious misunderstanding of the\par
\tab    nature of the paper-tape media.\par
\par
\tab    This flawed position is likely based on the irrelevant fact\par
\tab    that all products of the Typeset-8 group used premium quality\par
\tab    BRPE (\i Burpee\i0 ) paper-tape punches for six-level paper-tape\par
\tab    support using TTS code (the standard of the industry as used\par
\tab    by all Typeset-8 customers).  It would be bad for their \i image\par
\i0\tab    to admit that other DEC products were simply not made to the\par
\tab    same standards as was sold to the Typeset-8 customers.\par
\par
  \tab    This in part explains the presence of the (over-built) \par
\tab    Teletype Model 35 ASR terminals everywhere in the Typeset-8\par
\tab    development labs which occasionally were subject to visits by\par
\tab    various customer personnel.  Visitors generally had only\par
\tab    superficial understanding of the various hardware components\par
\tab    but gained confidence in Typeset-8 by first impressions.\par
\par
\tab    Teletype Model 35 ASR terminals look quite rugged (but still\par
\tab    may have read errors).  The PC04 readers generally attached to\par
\tab    many PDP-8 systems do not inspire the same level of visual\par
\tab    confidence.\par
\par
\tab    This was also coupled with the false notion of claiming the\par
\tab    dominant usage was for relatively small patches related to\par
\tab    custom changes for the benefit of end-user systems.\par
\par
\tab    We will likely never know if any development time was wasted\par
\tab    by the Typeset-8 group consequent to this poor choice of\par
\tab    manual data transport, as Typeset-8 group labs were generally\par
\tab    located far from either more general PDP-8 development areas\par
\tab    or TOPS-10 time-sharing system terminals.\par
\par
\tab    More to the point, DEC management for all 12-bit systems never\par
\tab    expected anything to be changed within the Typeset-8 group\par
\tab    since, at the time, it was perceived as the only cost center\par
\tab    producing a profit while the rest of the software development\par
\tab    and related groups were unable to show any equivalent addition\par
\tab    to the corporate bottom line.\page\tab    Note: It is the author's opinion that much of what passed for\par
\tab    productivity in the Typeset-8 group would more correctly be\par
\tab    described as make-work (or \i goldbricking\i0 ).  (There is ample\par
\tab    evidence of this obtained by examining the output of the OS/8\par
\tab    PTP: handler when text files are processed; at the time, the\par
\tab    only beneficial usage of this special handling was offline\par
\tab    printing on the Teletype Model 35 ASR terminals.)\par
\par
\ul 2.63 Proposed remedies to the DTORG problem\ulnone\par
\par
Apparently due to internal DEC politics, no attempt was made to remedy this problem by educating the Typeset-8 programmers regarding their naive design; various well-known mechanisms can easily solve the problem as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) Instead of implementing the DTORG directive as a violation \tab\tab    of the internal binary format of DEC BIN utilities and OS/8,\par
\tab    the binary data can be patterned after a variety of utility\par
\tab    formats such as certain details of P?S/8 non-system device\par
 \tab    handlers and all OS/8 device handlers (as required by OS/8\par
\tab    \i BUILD\i0 ).\par
\par
\tab    An arbitrary series of origin settings can be used to indicate\par
\tab    the intention of DTORG directive statements as follows:\par
\par
\tab    DTORG  1234               /DESIGNATE BLOCK 1234 FOR OUTPUT.\par
\par
\tab    This DTORG mechanism would create the equivalent of the\par
\tab    following binary output:\par
\par
        PREV=  .\tab\tab\tab\tab /NEED THE PREVIOUS ORIGIN SETTING.\par
\par
        *7777;*7777;*1234;*PREV\tab /OUTPUT FLAG ORIGINS FOLLOWED BY\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /THE DTORG BLOCK NUMBER FOLLOWED\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /BY THE PREVIOUS ORIGIN.\par
\par
\tab    A special-purpose loader is still required to create Typset-8\par
\tab    system blocks in the proposed format; however, the binary data\par
\tab    created is compatible with all loading mechanisms; a lengthy\par
\tab    binary paper-tape punched out and then read on a Teletype\par
\tab    Model 35 ASR read station could be known to have passed the\par
\tab    standard checksum test.  Afterwards, all designated blocks can\par
\tab    be written out at once (assuming all block images can be\par
\tab    simultaneously loaded into memory).\par
\par
\tab    Note: The Typeset-8 system runs on 4K machines.  The binary\par
\tab    paper-tape output created for a customized user configuration\par
\tab    would tend to be somewhat longer than the binary paper-tape\par
\tab    for \ul FOCAL, 1969\ulnone  (as an arbitrary but likely representative\par
\tab    example of comparable size, nearly the entire user memory\par
\tab    space of 00000-07577).  It is entirely possible far longer\par
\tab    tapes could be required depending on how much of the system\par
\tab    was being updated at any one time.\page\tab b) The example remedy represents an expedient method to solve the\par
\tab    DTORG problem.  More elaborate schemes could include\par
\tab    additional origin settings, perhaps following the last two\par
\tab    data-related origins as shown in the example.  An overall\par
\tab    mini-checksum for the entire string of origin settings could\par
\tab    be created if desired.  (The final origin must repeat the\par
\tab    value in effect prior to the DTORG grouping to allow proper\par
\tab    loading.)\par
\par
\tab    Regardless of specific implementation details, the entire\par
\tab    problem of supporting incompatible binary files would be\par
\tab    eliminated.  All binary files can be used with all available\par
\tab    binary format utilities.  After punching the lengthy paper-\par
\tab    tape, it could be read back using the high-speed paper-tape\par
\tab    reader.  The resulting file would not only have to pass the\par
\tab    standard checksum test, but additionally could be directly\par
\tab    compared to the original binary output file to completely\par
\tab    confirm the validity of the paper-tape before bringing the\par
\tab    tape to the Typeset-8 back-room facility (usually located in a\par
\tab    remote building) for the purpose of generating Typeset-8\par
\tab    system components.\par
\par
\tab c) Without having to support data in an incompatible binary\par
        format, the need to store paper-tape binary frames becomes\par
        superfluous.  OS/8 could have easily been developed using a\par
        more efficient internal file format similar to P?S/8 and the\par
        \ul\i R-L Monitor System\ulnone\i0  which would essentially be a 256 word\par
\tab    equivalent of Slurp binary loader format.\par
\par
\tab    It is well known to the author (and certain original DEC staff \tab    members), that DEC managers perverted certain aspects of the\par
        demo project that was eventually to become OS/8.  Their\par
\tab    demonstrable ignorance of PDP-8 operating systems has left its\par
\tab    mark on various forms of internal poor design aspects of that\par
\tab    system.\par
\par
\tab    It is surmised that Richard Lary intended to use 256 word\par
\tab    Slurp binary loader format for OS/8 (having just recently\par
\tab    demonstrated the \ul\i R-L Monitor System\ulnone\i0  to the same \ul\i dim bulb\ulnone\i0 s who\par
\tab    rejected that pioneering system), but the edict to use images\par
 \tab    of binary paper-tape frames clearly was created in part due to\par
\tab    ignorant (and arrogant) demands of the Typeset-8 group.\par
\par
\tab    Note: PAL10 also implements the creation of incompatible\par
\tab    binary paper-tapes; the DTORG support in PAL10 is identical to\par
\tab    that of PAL8 and actually was accomplished prior to the\par
        existence of OS/8.  However, since PAL10 is a cross-assembler,\par
        this has no impact on any aspect of the TOPS10 operating\par
        system performance; in OS/8 this poor design decision causes a\par
        noticeable loss of performance every time binary files are\par
        loaded by any OS/8 user.\page\tab d) OS/8 \i PIP\i0  could easily be modified to convert compatible binary\par
\tab    files using DTORG directives (as proposed) into Typeset-8\par
\tab    format binary paper-tapes (assuming lack of cooperation from\par
\tab    the Typeset-8 group).  Thus, the problems associated with\par
 \tab    incompatible binary format would only affect the developers of\par
\tab    Typeset-8, and not the entire OS/8 community at large.\par
\par
\tab    While not likely to occur, the binary format of OS/8 utilities\par
\tab    could be updated to 256 word Slurp binary loader format which\par
\tab    would improve the performance of OS/8 for all users.\par
\par
\tab e) While the DTORG directive is not useful for OS/8 purposes\par
 \tab    (every aspect of the present DTORG implementation is\par
\tab    responsible for some negative aspect of OS/8 performance),\par
\tab    since it addresses 128 logical words/block storage (while OS/8\par
\tab    addresses 256 word logical records), it can be useful for\par
\tab    future implementation of P?S/8 system generation utilities.\par
\tab    This would follow the overall flow of Typeset-8 system\par
\tab    generation methods (without any of the flawed specifics).\par
\par
\tab    Future releases of P?S/8 PAL (and the SHELL assembler PALX or\par
\tab    XPAL) will develop a more useful implementation of the DTORG\par
\tab    directive; for the present, P?S/8 PAL merely implements\tab\par
 \tab    DTORG as one of the nonfunctional directives supported for\tab\tab    source-level compatibility reasons, but generates an\par
\tab    incompatible subset of the proposed changes.\par
\par
\tab f) A P?S/8 utility, tentatively to be known as \i BINCON\i0 , may be\par
 \tab    written to read and convert present OS/8 binary files to the\par
 \tab    proposed format (with respect to the DTORG disposition); the\par
 \tab    resulting compatible binary format would then be stored as\par
\tab    P?S/8 Slurp format binary files.  This would implement binary\par
\tab    compatibility with the proposed changes to DTORG as will be\par
\tab    implemented in future releases of P?S/8 PAL.  P?S/8 system\par
\tab    generation utilities could then operate with binary paper\par
\tab    tapes or other media developed on either operating system.\line\line\tab    Since it is intended that P?S/8 BINCON operate in a\par
\tab    bi-directional manner (analogous to the OS8CON text conversion\par
\tab    utility), OS/8 users can benefit from the advanced assembly\par
\tab    features of P/S/8 PAL when loading (resulting) binary files in\par
\tab    OS/8.\par
\par
\tab    Note: P?S/8 BIN can create binary paper tapes in DEC BIN\par
 \tab    format on the low-speed or high-speed punch; this can be\par
\tab    accomplished by a chained \ul\i assemble-and-punch\ulnone\i0  operation as\par
 \tab    described in section 2.30 above.\par
\par
\tab    More information regarding P?S/8 Slurp binary file format is\par
\tab    available in the document \ul\i P?S/8 Binary File Internal\par
\ulnone\i0\tab    \ul\i Description\ulnone\i0  available separately.\page\ul 2.70 Additional information\par
\par
\ulnone P?S/8 PAL supports all mathematical operations commonly available in\par
most PDP-8 assemblers (and several operations that are not available in competing assembler programs) as follows:\line\par
\tab a) Unary operations.\par
\f1\par
\tab\tab VALUE= 100\tab\tab\tab /SET VALUE TO 0100.\par
\par
\tab\tab VALUE\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS 0100.\par
\par
\tab\tab +VALUE\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS 0100.\par
\par
\tab\tab -VALUE\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS 7700.\par
\par
\tab b) expressions.\par
\par
\tab\tab VAL1=   40\tab\tab\tab /SET FIRST VALUE TO 0040.\par
\par
\tab\tab VAL2=  100\tab\tab\tab /SET SECOND VALUE TO 0100.\par
\par
\tab\tab VAL1+1\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS 0041.\par
\par
\tab\tab VAL2-1\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS 0077.\par
\par
\tab\tab VAL1^4\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS 0200.\par
\par
\tab\tab VAL2%4\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS 0020.\par
\par
\tab\tab VAL1+VAL2\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS 0140.\par
\par
\tab\tab VAL1-VAL2\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS 7740.\par
\par
\tab\tab VAL2!-VAL1\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS 7740.\par
\par
 \tab\tab RDE;\tab 7\\VAL2\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS 0702 FOLLOWED BY 7100.\par
\par
Notes:\par
\par
\tab 1) While it is completely correct to use unary operations within \tab    compound expressions, cases such as the one shown above are\par
\tab    known to fail when using OS/8 PAL8 due to misimplementation\par
\tab    (contrary to OS/8 documentation).  As such, it may be\par
\tab    necessary to perform such operations in stages using\par
\tab    intermediate equations when there is a requirement to maintain\par
\tab    PAL8 compatibility.\par
\par
\tab 2) Multiply is signified by using the ^ operator.\par
\par
\tab 3) Divide is signified by using the % operator.\page\tab 4) When using the ! operator in expressions, the results are\par
\tab    obtained by performing an inclusive .OR. operation on the\par
\tab    elements given.  If the /Y command-line option switch is set,\par
\tab    the operation performed is changed to left-shift six bits.\par
\par
\tab 5) The last example is used when programming LINCtape operations\par
\tab    on a classic LINC or LINC-8 or PDP-12 system that supports the\par
\tab    corresponding hardware.  \i RDE\i0  is a two-word instruction to read\par
\tab    a LINCtape block; the second word is a compound word\par
\tab    containing the memory segment (quarter of a memory field) for\par
\tab    the transfer in the high order three bits and the block number\par
\tab    in the low-order nine bits.  The RDE symbol is already present \tab    in P?S/8 PAL if dual-mode assembly is enabled by use of either\par
\tab    the /8 or /9 command-line option switches.\par
\par
\tab 6) The \\ operator performs a left-shift nine bits on the supplied\par
\tab    value.  This is generally used to place the memory segment\par
\tab    bits into the second word of a two-word LINCtape read or write\par
\tab    instruction.\par
\par
\tab    P?S/8 PAL only supports the \\ operator if dual-mode assembly\par
\tab    is enabled by use of either the /8 or /9 command-line option\par
\tab    switches.\par
