\tab\tab The next line may be blank or consist of comments only \tab\tab\tab rendering the conditional moot.  During the early \tab\tab\tab\tab development of a program, this may deliberately be done to \tab\tab\tab force a specific outcome.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: If additional statements are on the same line as the \tab\tab\tab ASMIFZ\{ERO\} directive (using \highlight3 ;\highlight0  to separate the statements)\par
\tab\tab and the conditional test fails, the additional statements\par
\tab\tab are also ignored.\par
\par
ASMSKP \ul expression\ulnone\par
\par
\tab\tab The ASMSKP directive is one of the conditional assembly \tab\tab\tab features of the extended PAL language.  See Note 1.\par
\par
\tab\tab The specified \ul expression\ulnone  is evaluated as an unsigned 12-bit \tab\tab integer value.  The next \ul\i expression-value\ulnone\i0  lines of the \tab\tab\tab source code are ignored.\par
\par
\tab\tab ASMSKP  3\tab\tab\tab\tab /SKIP THE NEXT THREE LINES.\par
\tab\tab HLT\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS IGNORED!\par
\tab\tab JMP\tab    .+3\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS ALSO IGNORED!\par
\tab\tab /\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab IGNORED AND ALSO A COMMENT!\par
\par
\tab\tab If the \ul expression\ulnone  evaluates to zero, all lines following the \tab\tab ASMSKP directive will be assembled.  If the \ul expression\ulnone  \tab\tab\tab evaluates to a non-zero value and additional statements are\par
 \tab\tab on the same line as the ASMSKP directive (using \highlight3 ;\highlight0  to \tab\tab\tab\tab separate the statements), the additional statements are also \tab\tab ignored.\par
\par
\tab\tab The ASMSKP directive is needed because of the complex nature\par
\tab      of conditional assembly using the available set of related\par
\tab      directives (certain logical expressions might require the\par
\tab      use of multiple conditionals).  Unfortunately, since the\par
\tab      ASMSKP directive does not work with any form of delimiter\par
\tab      mechanism, the programmer must carefully count the lines\par
\tab      required to be skipped; slight changes to the program source\par
\tab      can cause unexpected results.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
CONSOL\{E\}\par
\par
\tab\tab The front panel switches are read during the assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab The CONSOL\{E\} directive returns the contents of the front \tab\tab\tab panel switches to allow storage of the value into an \tab\tab\tab\tab assembly-time symbol:\par
\par
\tab\tab SWITS=  CONSOLE\tab\tab /STORE CONSOLE SWITCHES IN SWITS.\par
\par
\tab\tab Conditional assembly techniques can then be used on the\par
\tab      resultant symbolic value for any purpose as determined by\par
\tab      the programmer (such as causing PAUSE directive output to\par
\tab      appear during the assembly).\par
\par
DATE\par
\par
\tab\tab The DATE directive returns the low-order 12-bits of the \tab\tab\tab current system date to allow storage of the value into an \tab\tab\tab assembly-time symbol:\par
\par
\tab\tab CURDAY= DATE\tab\tab\tab /STORE THE DATE IN CURDAY.\par
\par
\tab\tab Conditional assembly can use the date for a variety of \tab\tab\tab purposes including creating a current version of the \tab\tab\tab\tab program.  As of P?S/8 Version 8Z, the format of the returned\par
\tab\tab expression matches that used by current P?S/8 System\par
\tab\tab programs (which requires periodic program maintenance,\par
\tab\tab albeit infrequently).\par
\par
\tab\tab Future releases of P?S/8 PAL will likely include the \i DATEHI\par
\tab\tab\i0 directive to obtain the high-order six bits of the current\par
\tab\tab system date.  The two values can be combined to form the\par
\tab\tab complete date (in the range of 01-Jan-1900 through\par
\tab\tab 31-Dec-2411).  The proposed format will require alternate\par
\tab\tab calculations but will be free of the need for maintenance.\par
\par
\tab\tab More details regarding the specifics of calculating the\par
\tab\tab current system date can be found in \f0 the \ul P?S/8 Keyboard\par
\ulnone\tab\tab\ul Monitor Command Guide\ulnone  available as a separate document.\par
\f1\par
DECIMA\{L\}\par
\par
\tab\tab The internal radix for mathematical expressions is set to \tab\tab\tab decimal; the default radix is octal (which can be restored\par
\tab\tab by subsequent use of the OCTAL directive).\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
DEVICE\par
\par
\tab\tab The DEVICE directive takes a four-character string argument. \tab\tab It is used to form the device-name string used to reference \tab\tab P?S/8 non-system device handlers and all OS/8 device\par
\tab      handlers as follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab DEVICE  DTA1\tab\tab\tab /REFERENCE TO DTA1:\par
\par
\tab\tab The DEVICE directive creates two 12-bit words containing the \tab\tab packed six-bit ASCII text values of the argument.  In \tab the\par
\tab\tab example given, the output would be equivalent to 0424 (DT)\par
\tab\tab followed by 0161 (A1).\par
\par
\tab\tab The string argument is not processed in the same manner as\par
\tab\tab in the case of TEXT and related directives; as such, the /J\par
\tab\tab command-line option switch has no effect on DEVICE\par
\tab\tab directive usage.\par
\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1 DTORG \ul expression\ulnone\par
\par
\tab\tab The DTORG directive is intended to provide target storage\par
\tab\tab device block number information when generating operating\par
\tab\tab system components for Typeset-8 as follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab DTORG   60\tab\tab\tab /WRITE THIS PAGE TO BLOCK 0060.\par
\par
\tab\tab The \ul expression\ulnone  is evaluated as a 12-bit unsigned integer to \tab\tab designate a block number on a device such as TC01/TC08\par
\tab\tab DECtape.  Up to 128 words (assembled at the current location\par
\tab\tab counter) follow as the intended data to be written to the\par
\tab\tab target block.  See Notes 1, 2 and 3.\par
\par
\tab\tab The implementation of the DTORG directive is extremely\par
\tab\tab problematic.  See sections 2.61, 2.62 and 2.63 of this\par
\tab\tab document for more information.\par
\par
EJECT \ul\i optional-string-value\cf3\ulnone\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab\cf0 The EJECT directive causes assembler listing output to \tab\tab\tab advance to the next page.\par
\par
\tab\tab If used with the \ul optional-string-value\ulnone , the title on the \tab\tab\tab next page is set to the \ul optional-string-value\ulnone  text (subject \tab\tab to truncation).\par
\par
\tab\tab All statements containing the EJECT directive are not\par
 \tab\tab shown in the assembler listing output.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf0\par
\par
ENBITS\par
\par
\tab\tab The ENBITS directive is used in PAL10 to enable the bitmap\par
\tab\tab of the latest memory field when either a new FIELD\par
 \tab\tab directive is used or the end of the assembly.  This feature \tab\tab is not implemented in other PAL assemblers; the ENBITS \tab\tab\tab directive is provided for source-code compatibility purposes\par
\tab\tab only.  See Note 2.\par
\par
ENDBIN\par
\par
\tab\tab The ENDBIN directive closes the current binary file.  \tab\tab\tab\tab Additional binary output (if any) will be stored in the next \tab\tab binary output file.\par
\par
\tab\tab The ENDBIN directive is ignored if binary output\par
\tab\tab generation is disabled.\par
\par
\tab\tab The ENDBIN directive can be used to create additional\par
\tab\tab (optional) program segments ancillary to a master binary\par
\tab\tab file(s) to be initially loaded separately.  ENDBIN is\par
\tab\tab supported primarily for compatibility with the PAL III\par
\tab\tab paper-tape system assembler.\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
ENPUNC\{H\}\par
\par
\tab\tab Binary output generation may have been turned off using the\par
\tab\tab NOPUNC\{H\} directive; the ENPUNC\{H\} directive will re-enable\par
\tab\tab binary output generation.\par
\par
\tab\tab The NOPUNC\{H\} directive is often used when changing the\par
\tab\tab binary current location counter to a value different from\par
\tab\tab the (unrelocated) current location counter used during the\par
\tab\tab assembly.  This is used to relocate a code section that\par
\tab\tab initially loads into one memory area to another memory area\par
\tab\tab (by unstated means); the initial loading area is the current\par
\tab\tab location counter defined prior to the use of the NOPUNC\{H\}\par
\tab\tab directive.  The ENPUNC\{H\} directive is used to restore\par
\tab\tab binary output once the intended target current location\par
\tab\tab counter value is in effect.\par
\par
\tab\tab The ENPUNC\{H\} directive is ignored if binary output is\par
\tab\tab already enabled.  No error message will occur; this allows\par
\tab\tab ENPUNC\{H\} directive statements to be used to ensure binary\par
\tab\tab output has been restored.\par
\par
  \tab\tab The ENPUNC\{H\} directive is ignored if binary output\par
\tab\tab generation is disabled.  See Note 4.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
ERROR \ul\i optional-numeric-expression\ulnone\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The ERROR directive causes a deliberate error (and error \tab\tab\tab message).  If automatic chaining to P?S/8 BIN is in\par
\tab\tab effect (binary output files are being generated and the /G\par
\tab\tab command-line option switch is set), the chaining operation\par
\tab\tab will be prevented.\par
\par
\tab\tab Well developed PDP-8 programs can contain safeguards to \tab\tab\tab prevent undesirable scenarios such as having a definition \tab\tab\tab fall out of a required narrow range of addresses (or in some \tab\tab cases a unique required value).  This may help prevent\par
\tab      future program maintainers from committing logical blunders.\par
\par
  \tab\tab The value of the \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone  will be \tab\tab\tab\tab displayed as part of the error message; this may help\par
\tab\tab pinpoint the programming problem such as in the example that\par
\tab\tab follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab *7\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /APPROPRIATE STARTING LOCATION.\par
\par
 LOCTM1, .-.\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /TEMPORARY STORAGE.\par
 LOCTM2, .-.\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /ANOTHER TEMPORARY (WRONG PLACE!)\par
\par
\tab\tab AUTOXR= .\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS WRONG! SHOULD BE 0010.\par
\par
\tab\tab MYPTR=  AUTOXR+7\tab\tab /USED AS AN AUTO-INDEX REGISTER.\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNZRO  MYPTR&7770-10\tab <ERROR MYPTR>\par
\par
\tab\tab The value of \i MYPTR\i0  is 0020 instead of staying within the \tab\tab\tab required range of 0010 through 0017; as such, it is not \tab\tab\tab capable of being used as an auto-index register (as the \tab\tab\tab program logic demands).  Hopefully, the error message\par
\tab\tab\highlight3 ER  0020  AT LOCTM2+0001 (0011)\highlight0  (where 0011 is the current \tab\tab\tab location counter at the point of error in this example), is\par
\tab\tab sufficient to aid maintenance programmers in correcting \tab\tab\tab the mistake.  (The \i LOCTM2\i0  location is inappropriately placed \tab\tab in the auto-index register area ruining the program logic. \tab\tab\tab By moving LOCTM2 to another area of page-zero memory, the \tab\tab\tab problem is eliminated; program reassembly will not trigger \tab\tab\tab the error-detection safeguard.)\par
\par
\tab\tab Use of the ERROR directive in this manner can be further\par
\tab\tab embellished if desired.  See the description of the PAUSE\par
\tab\tab directive elsewhere in this document for further details.\par
\par
\tab\tab If the \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone  is not used, the error\par
\tab\tab message will be \highlight3 ER  0000\highlight0 .\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\par
\cf0 EXPUNG\{E\}\par
\par
\tab\tab The EXPUNG\{E\} directive is used to clear the entire symbol \tab\tab\tab table at the start of an assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab All permanent symbols are removed (except directives).  The \tab\tab program must define all symbols used (including MRI symbols \tab\tab using the FIXMRI directive).\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
\tab      By deleting the internal symbol table, it is possible that\par
\tab      certain programs may be capable of assembly on system\par
\tab      configurations with smaller memory size.\par
\par
\tab\tab As an example of proper usage of the EXPUNG\{E\} directive,\par
\tab\tab the source code of P?S/8 PAL performs an EXPUNG\{E\} at the\par
\tab\tab beginning of the assembly; as such, P?S/8 PAL fully defines\par
\tab\tab all symbols used within the program.  This guarantees proper\par
\tab\tab program generation by any qualifying assembler program (that\par
\tab\tab properly supports all required features).\par
\par
\tab\tab As of this writing, only recent implementations of P?S/8 PAL\par
\tab\tab and OS/8 PAL8 support all of the features required for\par
\tab\tab proper assembly of the P?S/8 PAL source code.  It is hoped\par
\tab\tab that savvy TOPS-10 programmers can modernize PAL10 to add\par
\tab\tab the few missing features in the current implementation.  (To\par
\tab\tab avoid consequential problems, many features of P?S/8 PAL are\par
\tab\tab not used in the P?S/8 PAL source code; where required, the\par
\tab\tab PQS directive is used to produce proper results should the\par
\tab\tab assembly be performed on an alternate assembler program.)\par
\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 PAL supports many symbols not ordinarily found in\par
\tab\tab other assemblers (such as IOT instructions for several PDP-8\par
\tab\tab peripherals and the full PDP-8/E \ul\i Extended Arithmetic Element\par
\ulnone\i0\tab\tab [\i EAE\i0 ]).\par
\par
\tab\tab Future releases of P?S/8 will include the optional SHELL\par
\tab\tab overlay; this will include an assembler (partially) based on\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 PAL to be known as PALX (or perhaps XPAL).  Either\par
\tab\tab P?S/8-based assembler program will be capable of assembling \tab\tab the source code of either program (although the SHELL-based\par
\tab\tab assembler will support additional features beyond the scope\par
\tab\tab of assembling the assembler).\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf0\par
\par
FIELD \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone\par
\par
\tab\tab The FIELD directive is used to set a designated memory field \tab\tab for binary loading of the statements that follow.  If the\par
 \tab\tab\ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone  is present, it is evaluated to \tab\tab\tab set the desired field; if there is no argument, the next \tab\tab\tab memory field past the present value is selected.\par
\par
\tab\tab When a FIELD directive is used, all open literal pools are \tab\tab\tab output and reset to default values.  If the /0 command-line\par
\tab      option switch is set, an automatic *200 will also be\par
\tab      generated overriding the previous value of the current\par
\tab      location counter.\par
\par
\tab\tab Memory fields up to a maximum of 256K are supported for\par
\tab\tab compatibility with the KT8A option (which supports\par
\tab      addressing memory up to 128K); however, all standard binary\par
\tab      loading utilities can only load binary data generated in\par
\tab      the 32K address space (FIELD 0 through FIELD 7).\par
\par
\tab      Future releases of P?S/8 will include an extended binary\par
\tab\tab loader that will require the KT8A hardware to operate; due\par
\tab\tab to complexity, an extended binary loader will operate in a\par
\tab\tab manner analogous to the current virtual Slurp loader.\par
\par
FILENA\{ME\}\par
\par
\tab\tab The FILENA\{ME\} directive takes a string argument in the form\par
\tab\tab of an (up to) six-character file name and a (one or) two-\par
\tab\tab character (optional) file extension.  This is used to create\par
\tab\tab a four word text string consisting of an OS/8 six-character\par
\tab\tab file name and a two-character file extension as follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab FILENAME FOOBAR.PA\tab\tab /STATIC FILE NAME STRING.\par
\par
\tab\tab The FILENA\{ME\} directive creates four 12-bit words\par
\tab\tab consisting of the packed six-bit ASCII text characters\par
\tab\tab derived from the string argument.  In the example given, the\par
\tab\tab output would consist of 0617 (FO), 1702 (OB), 0122 (AR) and\par
\tab\tab 2001 (PA).\par
\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 PAL supports the FILENA\{ME\} directive for OS/8 PAL8\par
\tab\tab compatibility.  Future P?S/8 systems will support the P?S/8\par
\tab\tab SHELL with an extended assembler based on P?S/8 PAL to be\par
\tab\tab designated PALX (or XPAL).  PALX will support an option to\par
\tab\tab extend the FILENA\{ME\} directive to support SHELL directory\par
\tab\tab file names (which are more complex than OS/8 file names).\par
\par
\tab\tab The string argument is not processed in the same manner as\par
\tab\tab in the case of TEXT and related directives; as such, the /J\par
\tab\tab command-line option switch has no effect on FILENA\{ME\}\par
\tab\tab directive usage.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
FIXMRI\par
\par
\tab\tab The FIXMRI directive is used to augment a standard equate \tab\tab\tab statement to process the symbol definition as a \ul\i Memory\par
\ulnone\i0\tab\tab R\ul\i eference Instruction\ulnone\i0  (\i MRI\i0 ).  MRI symbols modify the \tab\tab\tab\tab expression evaluation so that the PDP-8 addressing rules are \tab\tab applied instead of \ul\i inclusive or\ulnone\i0  (.\i IOR\i0 .) processing (which is \tab\tab the default method of expression evaluation) as follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab FIXMRI  INC=\tab 2000\tab\tab /ISZ PRESUMED TO NEVER SKIP.\par
\tab\par
\tab\tab The FIXMRI directive is also used to define floating-point \tab\tab\tab pseudo-instructions that generally follow the MRI addressing \tab\tab rules as part of several software floating-point packages.\par
\par
\tab\tab When the EXPUNG\{E\} directive is used, FIXMRI is needed to \tab\tab\tab redefine all required MRI symbols (such as \i AND\i0 , \i TAD\i0 , \i ISZ\i0 ,\par
\tab\tab\i DCA\i0 ,\i  JMS \i0 and \i JMP\i0 ).\par
\par
FIXTAB\par
\par
\tab\tab The FIXTAB directive is used after all symbols meant to be\par
\tab\tab considered as an extension of the permanent symbol table are\par
\tab\tab (fully) defined.\par
\par
\tab\tab All such initialization should occur at the start of the\par
\tab\tab assembly to establish the permanent symbol table (perhaps\par
\tab\tab after the use of the EXPUNG\{E\} directive).\par
\par
\tab\tab All permanent symbols will not be included in the cross-\tab\tab\tab reference output (if the /X command-line option switch is in\par
\tab      effect) or the symbol table printout at the end of the\par
\tab      assembly (if the /S command-line option switch is in effect)\par
\tab      unless the /A command-line option switch is (also) set.\par
\par
I\par
\par
\tab\tab The I directive is required in all PDP-8 MRI statements\par
\tab\tab using indirect references.  This forces bit[3] to be set so\par
\tab\tab the generated instruction value to conforms to the PDP-8\par
\tab\tab addressing rules.  This is done after the evaluated\par
\tab\tab expression is adjusted according to the MRI addressing rules\par
\tab\tab in effect.\par
\par
\tab\tab Indirect statements using the I directive are subject to\par
\tab\tab errors caused by violation of the PDP-8 addressing rules.\par
\tab\tab See Note 5.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf0\par
\par
IFDEF \ul\i symbol\ulnone\i0  \highlight3 <\highlight0\ul\i statements\highlight3\ulnone\i0 >\highlight0\par
\par
\tab\tab The IFDEF directive is one of the conditional assembly \tab\tab\tab features of the PAL language.\par
\par
\tab\tab The symbol table is searched for the specified \ul symbol\ulnone .  If\par
\tab\tab the \ul symbol\ulnone  is present (and defined), the \ul statements\par
\ulnone\tab\tab contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are assembled.  If the \ul symbol\ulnone  \tab\tab\tab is not present (or not defined) at this point in the\par
\tab\tab assembly, the \ul statements\ulnone  contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are\par
\tab\tab ignored.\par
\par
\tab\tab Unlike ordinary symbolic references, attempts to access the\par
\tab\tab specified \ul symbol\ulnone  while undefined (using the IFDEF directive)\par
\tab\tab will not cause \highlight3 U\highlight0 ndefined \highlight3 S\highlight0 ymbol errors.\par
\par
\tab\tab The \ul statements\ulnone  can span an indefinite number of lines of \tab\tab\tab source code as necessary.  Conditionals are occasionally \tab\tab\tab used within other conditionals in a \i nested\i0  manner.  The \tab\tab\tab count of  \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  must be carefully maintained to achieve an \tab\tab error-free assembly; the contents of comments are scanned\par
\tab\tab for \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  when conditional assembly is in effect.\par
\par
\tab\tab The IFDEF directive is often used to include other symbols \tab\tab\tab (or data) into an assembly when relevant:\par
\par
\tab\tab IFDEF   TC01\tab <\tab\tab /IF TC01/TC08 DECTAPE.\par
\par
\tab\tab NOPUNCH\tab\tab\tab\tab /NO BINARY OUTPUT FOR NOW.\par
\par
\tab\tab *7754\tab\tab\tab\tab /WHERE WC, CA ARE.\par
\par
 WC,\tab\tab .-.\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /WORD COUNT.\par
 CA,\tab\tab .-.\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /CURRENT ADDRESS.\par
\par
\tab\tab ENPUNCH\tab\tab\tab\tab /BINARY OUTPUT ON AGAIN.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab >\tab\tab /END OF CONDITIONAL.\par
\par
\tab\tab The controlling symbol \i TC01\i0  should be defined in an earlier\par
\tab\tab section of the source file where all global symbols are\par
\tab\tab generally grouped together as follows:\par
\par
 /\tab\tab GLOBAL SYMBOLS HERE.\par
\par
\tab\tab TC01=   1\tab\tab\tab\tab /USING TC01/TC08 DECTAPE.\par
\par
\tab\tab Since the controlling symbol DECTAP\{E\} is defined, the\par
\tab\tab conditional section shown above will be assembled.\page  \cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
IFNDEF \ul symbol\ulnone  \highlight3 <\highlight0\ul statements\highlight3\ulnone >\highlight0\par
\par
  \tab\tab The IFNDEF directive is one of the conditional assembly \tab\tab\tab features of the PAL language.\par
\par
\tab\tab The symbol table is searched for the specified \ul symbol\ulnone .  If\par
\tab\tab the \ul symbol\ulnone  is not present (or not defined), the \ul statements\par
\ulnone\tab\tab contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are assembled.  If the \ul symbol\ulnone  \tab\tab\tab is present (and defined) at this point in the assembly, the\par
\tab\tab\ul statements\ulnone  contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are ignored.\par
\par
\tab\tab Unlike ordinary symbolic references, attempts to access the\par
\tab\tab specified \ul symbol\ulnone  while undefined (using the IFNDEF \tab\tab\tab\tab directive) will not cause \highlight3 U\highlight0 ndefined \highlight3 S\highlight0 ymbol errors.\par
\par
\tab\tab The \ul statements\ulnone  can span an indefinite number of lines of \tab\tab\tab source code as necessary.  Conditionals are occasionally \tab\tab\tab used within other conditionals in a nested manner.  The \tab\tab\tab count of  \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  must be carefully maintained to achieve an \tab\tab error-free assembly; the contents of comments are scanned\par
\tab\tab for \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  when conditional assembly is in effect.\par
\par
\tab\tab The IFNDEF directive can be used to initialize a default\par
\tab\tab symbolic value as follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNDEF  NUDATA <\par
\par
\tab\tab NUDATA= 1\tab\tab  >\tab\tab /GET NEW DATA.\par
\par
\tab\tab In the example above, the conditional statement is assembled \tab\tab during pass one of the assembly.  During pass two the \tab\tab\tab\tab statement is ignored because the symbol is already defined.\par
\par
\tab\tab By defining a symbol in this manner, conditional control of\par
\tab\tab other statements located in other parts of the assembly may \tab\tab be created.  The example coding shown in the IFDEF directive\par
\tab\tab section above is typical of such statements.\par
\par
\tab\tab When multiple possibilities exist, a more complex set of\par
\tab\tab statements can chose a desired default as follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNDEF  DF32\tab <DF32= 0>\tab /ASSUME DF32 NOT PRESENT.\par
\tab\tab IFNDEF  RF08\tab <RF08= 0>\tab /ASSUME RF08 NOT PRESENT.\par
\tab\tab IFNDEF  TC01\tab <TC01= 0>\tab /ASSUME TC01 NOT PRESENT.\par
\tab\par
\tab\tab IFZERO  DF32+RF08+TC01 <TC01= 1>\tab /USE TC01 BY DEFAULT.\par
\par
\tab\tab A global definition near the beginning of the source file\par
\tab\tab can be used to override the (safety) default as used above.\par
\par
\tab\tab More information on the IFZERO directive is available\par
\tab\tab elsewhere in this document.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf0\par
\par
IFNZRO \ul expression\ulnone  \highlight3 <\highlight0\ul statements\highlight3\ulnone >\highlight0\par
\cf3\par
\cf0   \tab\tab The IFNZRO directive is one of the conditional assembly \tab\tab\tab features of the PAL language.\par
\par
\tab\tab The specified \ul expression\ulnone  is evaluated.  If the result is \tab\tab\tab non-zero, the \ul statements\ulnone  contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are \tab\tab\tab assembled.  If the result is zero, the \ul statements\ulnone  contained \tab\tab within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are ignored.\par
\par
\tab\tab The \ul statements\ulnone  can span an indefinite number of lines of \tab\tab\tab source code as necessary.  Conditionals are occasionally \tab\tab\tab used within other conditionals in a nested manner.  The \tab\tab\tab count of  \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  characters must be carefully maintained to\par
\tab      achieve an error-free assembly; the contents of comments are\par
\tab      scanned for \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  when conditional assembly is in effect.\par
\par
\tab\tab The IFNZRO directive is often used to check an assembly \tab\tab\tab parameter range as follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab *7\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /APPROPRIATE STARTING LOCATION.\par
\par
 LOCTM1,\tab .-.\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /TEMPORARY STORAGE.\par
 LOCTM2,\tab .-.\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /ANOTHER TEMPORARY (WRONG PLACE!)\par
\par
\tab\tab AUTOXR= .\tab\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS WRONG! SHOULD BE 0010.\par
\par
\tab\tab MYPTR=  AUTOXR+7\tab\tab /USED AS AN AUTO-INDEX REGISTER.\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNZRO  MYPTR&7770-10\tab <ERROR MYPTR>\par
\par
\tab\tab The value of MYPTR is 0020 instead of staying within the \tab\tab\tab required range of 0010 through 0017; as such, it is not \tab\tab\tab capable of being used as an auto-index register (as the \tab\tab\tab program logic demands).  An error message will be issued\par
\tab\tab (during pass two) when this code section is assembled:\par
\par
\tab\tab\highlight3 ER  0020  AT LOCTM2+0001 (0011)\par
\highlight0\par
\tab\tab Situations such as the example above often come about when\par
\tab\tab maintenance programmers do not completely understand the\par
\tab\tab design created by the original author(s).  Proper safeguards\par
\tab\tab can be designed in to prevent program logic blunders.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1 IFZERO \ul expression\ulnone  \highlight3 <\highlight0\ul statements\highlight3\ulnone >\highlight0\par
\cf3\par
\cf0   \tab\tab The IFZERO directive is one of the conditional assembly \tab\tab\tab features of the PAL language.\par
\par
\tab\tab The specified \ul expression\ulnone  is evaluated.  If the result is \tab\tab\tab zero, the \ul statements\ulnone  contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are\par
\tab\tab assembled.  If the result is non-zero, the \ul statements\par
\ulnone\tab\tab contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are ignored.\par
\par
\tab\tab The \ul statements\ulnone  can span an indefinite number of lines of \tab\tab\tab source code as necessary.  Conditionals are occasionally \tab\tab\tab used within other conditionals in a nested manner.  The \tab\tab\tab count of  \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  must be carefully maintained to achieve an \tab\tab error-free assembly; the contents of comments are scanned\par
\tab\tab for \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  when conditional assembly is in effect.\par
\cf3\par
\cf0\tab\tab The IFZERO directive is often used to create assembler \tab\tab\tab default values:\par
\par
\tab\tab IFZERO  OURFLD   <\par
\par
\tab\tab BUFFER= 4000\tab\tab\tab /USE FIELD 0 VALUE.>\par
\par
\tab\tab IFZERO  OURFLD-1 <\par
\par
\tab\tab BUFFER= 6000\tab\tab\tab /USE FIELD 1 VALUE.>\par
\par
\tab\tab In the above example, a parameter must be set to a value \tab\tab\tab consistent with an overall assembly parameter that can have \tab\tab multiple values.\par
\par
LMODE\par
\par
\tab\tab The LMODE directive is provided in the dual mode assembly \tab\tab\tab module to enable LINC mode addressing and certain symbols \tab\tab\tab unique to LINC mode.  See Note 1.\par
\par
NOBITS\par
\par
\tab\tab The NOBITS directive is used in PAL10 to disable the\par
 \tab\tab bitmap of the latest memory field when either a new FIELD \tab\tab\tab directive is used or the end of the assembly.  This feature \tab\tab is not implemented in any other PAL assembler; the NOBITS \tab\tab\tab directive is provided for compatibility purposes only.  See \tab\tab Note 2.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
NOPUNC\{H\}\par
\par
\tab\tab The NOPUNC\{H\} directive turns off binary output generation\par
\tab\tab during the assembly without affecting the assembler's\par
\tab\tab current location counter.  Using the ENPUNC\{H\} directive\par
\tab\tab later in the assembly will restore binary output.\par
\par
\tab\tab The NOPUNC\{H\} directive is often used when changing the\par
\tab\tab binary current location counter to a value different from\par
\tab\tab the (unrelocated) current location counter used during the\par
\tab\tab assembly.  This is used to relocate a code section that\par
\tab\tab initially loads into one memory area to another memory area\par
\tab\tab (by unstated means); the initial loading area is the current\par
\tab\tab location counter defined prior to the use of the NOPUNC\{H\}\par
\tab\tab directive.  The ENPUNC\{H\} directive is used to restore\par
\tab\tab binary output once the intended target current location\par
\tab\tab counter value is in effect.\par
\par
  \tab\tab The NOPUNC\{H\} directive is ignored if binary output \tab\tab\tab\tab generation is not enabled in the overall assembly.  See Note \tab\tab 4.\par
\par
OCTAL\par
\par
\tab\tab The internal radix for mathematical expressions is set to \tab\tab\tab octal.  While the default radix is octal, an earlier section \tab\tab of source code may have changed the radix to decimal using\par
\tab\tab the DECIMA\{L\} directive.  Any source code section where the\par
\tab\tab current radix is uncertain should include a statement using\par
\tab\tab the OCTAL directive to restore the default radix.\par
\par
PAGE \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone\par
\par
\tab\tab The PAGE directive is used to set an origin setting at the \tab\tab\tab lowest location on a PDP-8 memory page for binary loading of \tab\tab statements that follow.  If the \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone  \tab\tab is present, it is evaluated to set the desired page; if \tab\tab\tab there is no argument, the next memory page is selected.\par
\par
\tab\tab Selecting a new page will cause the output of the \tab\tab\tab\tab literal table of the previous page in effect (if any).\par
\par
\tab\tab The default origin of any assembly is 0200; this is\par
\tab\tab equivalent to using the \i PAGE 1\i0  statement  or a statement of\par
\tab\tab the form:\par
\par
\tab\tab *200\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /START AT 0200.\par
\par
\tab\tab Changing the current page will force a dump of current page\par
\tab\tab literals (assuming literal generation is in effect).\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
PAUSE \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone\par
\par
\tab\tab The PAUSE directive has two different usages:\par
\par
\tab\tab When used without the \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone , the PAUSE \tab\tab directive indicates the rest of the latest input file will \tab\tab\tab be ignored.  This usage of the PAUSE directive is provided \tab\tab\tab for compatibility with the paper-tape operating system PAL \tab\tab\tab III assembler.\par
\par
\tab\tab When used with the \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone , a PAUSE \tab\tab\tab message is displayed during each pass of the assembly.  This \tab\tab is a user-defined function and is not an error message per \tab\tab\tab se (although the programmer can define values to specific \tab\tab\tab messages to describe some internal situation related to an\par
\tab      error in an internal coding section).\par
\par
  \tab\tab When developing extremely long programs, programmers \tab\tab\tab\tab may use the PAUSE directive to signal the current program \tab\tab\tab section and/or the start (or end) of each assembly pass.\par
\tab\tab For more information see Section 2.03 of this document.\par
\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1 PMODE\par
\par
\tab\tab The PMODE directive is provided in the dual mode assembly \tab\tab\tab module to enable PDP-8 mode addressing and certain symbols \tab\tab\tab unique to PDP-8 mode.  See Note 1.\par
\par
\tab\tab When P?S/8 PAL is operating in PDP-8 mode, there is\par
\tab\tab generally no difference between dual-mode assembly and the\par
\tab\tab normal single-mode assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab For compatibility with programs developed using \f0 the embedded\par
\tab\tab assembler utility of the \ul\i LAP6-DIAL\ulnone\i0 /\ul\i DIAL-MS\ulnone\i0  operating system, \tab\tab certain additional directives are included when dual-mode \tab\tab\tab assembly is enabled in P?S/8 PAL. \par
\par
\tab\tab This includes an alternate set of conditional assembly \tab\tab\tab directives that are not available in any other PAL \tab\tab\tab\tab assembler.\par
\par
\f1 PQS\par
\par
\tab\tab The PQS directive is a feature unique P?S/8 PAL.  It can\par
 \tab\tab be used in conditional assembly statements to allow special-\tab\tab purpose programming only supported by P?S/8 PAL; alternate \tab\tab\tab statements can attempt to provide compatibility with other \tab\tab\tab assemblers.  Unfortunately, certain powerful features of\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 PAL are not available in other assemblers; crude \tab\tab\tab alternatives have been used to provide a functional (albeit \tab\tab inferior) result where possible.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf0\tab\par
\par
\tab\tab Features of P?S/8 PAL such as conditional literals have been \tab\tab instrumental in implementing several very large bootable\par
\tab      application programs requiring both storage devices and 32K\par
\tab      memory; conditional assembly has been used to provide\par
\tab      inferior support of statically-determined page zero literals\par
\tab      through the use of manual optimization at some point in the\par
\tab      development.  However, as additional development is\par
\tab      performed, the static manual optimization tends to drift\par
\tab      while assembly with P?S/8 is always current.\par
  \par
\tab\tab Only additional manual optimization can sync up the relevant\par
\tab      (newer) changes.  As such, the PQS directive has been\par
\tab      extensively used to allow PAL8 to replicate the latest\par
\tab\tab static optimization until further development with P?S/8 PAL\par
\tab\tab can be performed.\par
\par
\tab\tab The forthcoming P?S/8 SHELL will include a more advanced\par
\tab\tab assembler (partially) based on P?S/8 PAL tentatively to be\par
\tab\tab known as \i PALX\i0  (or perhaps \i XPAL\i0 ).  P?S/8 SHELL PALX will also\par
\tab\tab support the PQS directive; however, since XPAL will be a\par
\tab\tab more comprehensive assembler, a companion \i PQSX \i0 directive\par
\tab\tab will also be implemented.  This will allow programmers to\par
\tab\tab take advantage of the advanced features of both P?S/8\par
\tab\tab assemblers.\cf2\par
\cf0\par
RELOC \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone\par
\par
\tab\tab The RELOC directive performs much of the functionality of \tab\tab\tab the NOPUNC\{H\} and ENPUNC\{H\} directives in a more compact and \tab\tab elegant form.  The \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone  is evaluated \tab\tab to form a relocation factor that sets up an offset between \tab\tab\tab the assembler's current location counter and the binary \tab\tab\tab output origin.\par
\cf2\par
\cf0\tab\tab Note: Since the relocation factor is applied to all binary\par
\tab\tab output, the RELOC directive can be used in conjunction with \tab\tab literals.\par
\par
\tab\tab Additional usage of the RELOC directive with the\par
\i\tab\tab\ul\i0 optional-numeric-expression\ulnone  causes cumulative relocation, \tab\tab\tab which can be useful in certain obscure situations.  The \tab\tab\tab RELOC directive used without the \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone  \tab\tab resets the relocation factor to zero.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: While PAL8 is the only other assembler that supports\par
\tab\tab the RELOC directive, there are certain differences:\par
\par
\tab\tab a) PAL8 flags statements detected as \i apparently\i0  relocated\par
\tab\tab    with \highlight3 *\highlight0 .  P?S/8 PAL flags statements in a similar manner,\par
\tab\tab    but only if relocation is actually in effect.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab    If a statement of the form\par
\par
\tab\tab    RELOC .\par
\par
\tab\tab    is used in the source program, relocation is reset to\par
 \tab\tab    zero; however, PAL8 will only cancel the statement\par
\tab\tab    relocation flag when the RELOC directive is used\par
\tab\tab    without the \i optional-numeric-expression\i0 .\par
\par
\tab\tab b) PAL8 does not flag statements that are relocated by\par
\tab\tab    the use of the NOPUNC\{H\} and ENPUNC\{H\} directives.\par
\tab\tab    P?S/8 PAL outputs the \highlight3 *\highlight0  on all statements actually\par
\tab\tab    relocated regardless of method of relocation.\par
\par
\tab\tab    Note: Certain advanced programming methods include the\par
 \tab\tab    simultaneous usage of the NOPUNC\{H\} and ENPUNC\{H\}\par
\tab\tab    directives and the RELOC directive; this is necessary\par
\tab\tab    when there are several independent aspects of relocation\par
\tab\tab    in effect simultaneously.\cf2\par
\cf0\par
SEGMNT \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone\par
\par
\tab\tab The SEGMNT directive is used to set the field and origin \tab\tab\tab setting to the quarter memory field segment specified by the \tab\tab\i optional-numeric-expression\i0  in the range of 00 (field 0, \tab\tab\tab location 0000) through 37 (field 7,location 6000) as \tab\tab\tab\tab required by the LINC architecture of the LINC-8 and PDP-12.\par
\par
\tab\tab If the \i optional-numeric-expression\i0  is present, it is \tab\tab\tab\tab evaluated to set the desired segment; if there is no \tab\tab\tab\tab argument, the next memory segment is selected.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Segment 2 is the default value for LINC mode assembly \tab\tab followed by an origin of 0020 (04020).  This is equivalent \tab\tab\tab to SEGMNT 2 followed by *20 (in LINC mode, addresses are \tab\tab\tab specified within a segment).  See Note 1.\par
\par
SIXBIT \i text-string\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The SIXBIT directive creates 12-bit words packed with six-\tab\tab\tab bit characters taken from the \i text-string\i0  (other than \tab\tab\tab\tab delimiter characters).  The encoding is six-bit ASCII text \tab\tab\tab as used with similar directives in assemblers for DEC 18-bit \tab\tab and 36-bit systems (which is not the PDP-8 standard).\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: If the number of characters in the \i text-string\i0  is \tab\tab\tab even, an additional 12-bit word of 0000 is created as a \tab\tab\tab delimiter unless the /J command-line option switch is set.\par
\tab      For more information see Section 2.41 of this document.\page Appendix B (continued)\par
\par
SKIP \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone\par
\par
\tab\tab The SKIP directive is used to create deliberately blank \tab\tab\tab lines in the listing output of an assembly.  The\par
\tab\tab\i optional-numeric-expression\i0  is evaluated as an unsigned \tab\tab\tab 12-bit integer.  Blank lines are created as many times as \tab\tab\tab necessary unless the value is zero, in which case no blank \tab\tab\tab lines are created.  If the \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone  is\par
\tab\tab not present, one blank line will be created.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: All lines containing the SKIP directive will not be \tab\tab\tab displayed in the listing.\cf2\par
\cf0\par
TEXT \i text-string\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The TEXT directive creates 12-bit words packed with six-\tab\tab\tab bit characters taken from the \i text-string\i0  (other than \tab\tab\tab\tab delimiter characters).  The encoding is six-bit ASCII text \tab\tab\tab as typically used on the PDP-8 (which is not compatible with \tab\tab similar directives in assemblers for DEC 18-bit and 36-bit \tab\tab\tab systems).\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: If the number of characters in the \i text-string\i0  is \tab\tab\tab even, an additional 12-bit word of 0000 is created as a \tab\tab\tab delimiter unless the /J command-line option switch is set.\par
\tab      For more information see Section 2.41 of this document.\par
\par
TITLE \i text-string\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The TITLE directive is used to set the title field of the \tab\tab\tab page header used on every listing page if the /L and /N \tab\tab\tab command-line option switches are set.  The \i text-string\i0  will\par
\tab      be used (subject to truncation) starting with the next\par
\tab      printed page.\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
\tab\tab Normally, the title field is taken from the first line of \tab\tab\tab the first input file; the TITLE directive can only affect \tab\tab\tab subsequent output.  However, the TITLE directive can be used \tab\tab to override the first page title field by using the XLIST \tab\tab\tab directive beforehand:\par
\par
\tab\tab /\tab EXAMPLE WHERE THIS IS NOT USED IN THE TITLE FIELD.\par
\par
\tab\tab XLIST\tab\tab\tab\tab /TURN OFF LISTING NOW.\par
\par
\tab\tab TITLE   MY TITLE\tab\tab /USE DESIRED TITLE INSTEAD.\par
\par
\tab\tab XLIST\tab\tab\tab\tab /TURN LISTING ON NOW.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab      By use of the TITLE directive, the title field on the\par
\tab      listing page headers do not have to start with a "/"\par
\tab      character (which all comments must have).  All default\par
\tab      titles come from the first line of the file; a typical\par
\tab      statement would generally be a poor choice for a title\par
\tab      field.\cf2\par
\cf0\par
XLIST \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone\par
\par
\tab\tab The XLIST directive is used to hide portions of a listing \tab\tab\tab file (assuming the /L command-line option switch is set).\par
\tab      If the \ul optional-numeric-expression\ulnone  evaluates to a non-zero\par
\tab      value, the listing is turned off.  If it evaluates to zero,\par
\tab      the listing output is restored.  If no argument is given,\par
\tab      the listing output state is reversed from its former state.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: All lines containing the XLIST directive are hidden.\par
\par
Z\par
\par
\tab\tab The Z directive is provided for compatibility with the PAL \tab\tab\tab II assembler of the paper-tape operating system.  All PDP-8 \tab\tab assemblers starting with PAL III are capable of properly \tab\tab\tab setting arguments (or operands) to MRI class instructions\par
\tab      according to the rules of the PDP-8 architecture.  PAL II\par
\tab      only implements a subset where all symbols are erroneously\par
\tab      placed on the current page.  When necessary, the Z directive\par
\tab      is used on all statements that actually address Page Zero to\par
\tab      remove the current page bit from the generated value.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: PAL III was originally written in PAL II.  Later \tab\tab\tab releases of the source code were modified to require a \tab\tab\tab binary copy of PAL III to assemble its own source code.\par
\par
\tab\tab It is known that PAL8 ignores the Z directive while P?S/8 \tab\tab\tab PAL implements the original intention.\par
\par
ZBLOCK \i numeric-expression\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The ZBLOCK directive is used to create blocks of data set to \tab\tab 0000.  The \i numeric-expression\i0  is evaluated as an unsigned \tab\tab\tab integer; the value determines how many words are generated.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: If the \i numeric-expression\i0  evaluates to zero, no words \tab\tab are generated.\page\cf3 Appendix B Notes\par
\cf0\par
1)\tab Certain directives are only available if LINC mode dual assembly \tab is enabled using either the /8 or /9 command-line option\par
\tab switches.  They are provided primarily for compatibility with the\par
\tab DIAL-MS dual mode assembler.  Due to the nature of LINC mode\par
\tab expressions that use one's complement arithmetic, the programmer\par
\tab must be aware of the attendant quirks (such as positive and\par
\tab negative zero values).\par
\par
2)\tab Despite not being particularly associated with dual mode \tab assembly, certain directives are provided only if either the /8 \tab or /9 command-line option switches are set.  These\par
\tab directives will be moved to the general symbol table load in a\par
\tab future release to avoid this erroneous dependency; for the\par
\tab present, dual \tab mode assembly must be enabled.  Additional\par
\tab directives not currently supported may be added to a future\par
\tab release of P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
3)\tab The DTORG directive should not be used at this time as its \tab presence in the source code causes the binary output to be \tab corrupted.  See Sections 2.61, 2.62 and 2.63 of this document for\par
\tab more information.\par
\par
4)\tab The NOPUNC\{H\} and ENPUNC\{H\} directives are often used to relocate \tab binary output as part of an overlay structure.  The assembler \tab current location counter can be updated while the binary output \tab origin remains fixed.  The program segment loads into an address \tab space that can be moved or stored elsewhere while properly \tab assembled for its ultimate execution address space:\par
\par
\tab\tab *6000\tab\tab\tab /WHERE THE GENERATED CODE LOADS.\par
\par
\tab\tab NOPUNCH\tab\tab\tab /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.\par
\par
\tab\tab *7600\tab\tab\tab /WHERE THE CODE EXECUTES.\par
\par
\tab\tab ENPUNCH\tab\tab\tab /UN-FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.\par
\par
\tab Note: The NOPUNC\{H\} and ENPUNC\{H\} directives are traditional \tab features of several PDP-8 assemblers including PAL10; literals\par
\tab cannot be used with this form of relocation mechanism.\par
\par
\tab The RELOC directive can accomplish a similar form of code \tab relocation including literals if necessary.  However, it is only \tab supported by P?S/8 PAL and PAL8.\par
\par
5)\tab The I directive changes meaning if LINC mode assembly is in \tab effect.  Since there are no indirect references in LINC \tab programming, the I directive is redefined to force bit[7] on.  In \tab the context of LINC addressing, the I directive means either \tab immediate mode (when used with instructions where the operand \tab immediately follows the instruction) or auto-indexed mode (when \tab used with references to locations 0001-0017).\page\cf2 Appendix B Notes (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Many LINC instructions take immediate arguments:\par
\par
\tab\tab LMODE\tab\tab\tab /LINC MODE ASSEMBLY.\par
\par
\tab\tab LDA I;    3000\tab\tab /LOAD THE AC WITH 3000.\par
\tab\tab ADA  ;    FOO \tab\tab /ADD THE CONTENTS OF FOO\par
\par
\tab The first instruction above uses the I bit to indicate the \tab argument (3000) is immediately after the instruction.  The second \tab instruction does not use the I bit; as such, the address of the \tab operand follows (which ironically means it is an indirect \tab reference to the operand).\par
\par
\tab The PDP-8 references to locations 0010-0017 are auto-incremented \tab when referenced indirectly.  In LINC mode, references to 0001-\tab 0017 are ordinary indirect references unless the I directive is \tab used:\par
\par
\tab\tab LMODE\tab\tab\tab /LINC MODE ASSEMBLY.\par
\par
\tab\tab ADA    17\tab\tab\tab /ADD WHAT IS POINTED TO.\par
\tab\tab ADA I  17\tab\tab\tab /ADD NEXT VALUE AS WELL.\par
\par
\tab The first instruction uses the present contents of 0017 to point \tab to the operand.  The second instruction first increments the \tab contents of 0017 to point to the (next) operand.\par
\par
\tab Certain LINC instructions have special meaning for references to \tab location 0000; these instructions have specific addressing needs \tab which do not involve the I bit.  The auto-increment references to \tab locations 0001-0017 clearly are the inspiration for the PDP-8 \tab auto-increment usage of locations 0010-0017.  The range was \tab shortened to allow the PDP-8 interrupt handling vector at the\par
\tab lower addresses.  (The LINC was designed with a primitive\par
\tab interrupt structure; the LINC-8 does not support LINC\par
\tab interrupts.)\par
\par
\tab The PDP-12 defines LINC mode interrupts analogous to the PDP-8\par
\tab interrupt system (directed to an alternate vector location).\par
\par
\tab The PDP-8 cannot use indirect references to 0010-0017 without\par
\tab auto-increment; to this limited extent, the LINC instruction set\par
\tab is superior to the PDP-8 instruction set.  LINC mode addressing \tab does not allow indirect usage of any other locations (other than \tab pointer locations immediately after the instructions).  There is \tab no concept of a page zero in the LINC architecture, and there are \tab only three instructions that address all of memory directly. \tab However, all of memory in this case is limited to 1024 locations,\par
\tab not the PDP-8 norm of 4096 locations.\page\cf2 Appendix B Notes (continued)\par
\par
\tab\cf0 There is no concept of a subroutine call mechanism in the LINC\par
 \tab architecture; the \i JMP\i0  instruction attempts to act as a stand-in.\par
\tab However, if additional JMP instructions are used within the\par
\tab subroutine, various fixups (read \i kludges\i0 ) are required to protect\par
\tab the return address.\par
\par
\tab All features considered, system architecture experts are welcome\par
\tab to weigh in with their own opinions as to the relative worth of\par
\tab PDP-8 versus LINC architecture; their views may greatly differ\par
\tab from the author's viewpoint.\par
\par
\tab Few operating systems for the LINC architecture support PDP-8\par
\tab programming; all known implementations are quite inferior to the\par
\tab various PDP-8 assemblers mentioned elsewhere in this document.\par
\tab To the author's knowledge, none of these systems support mixed\par
\tab dual mode assembly to any extent; rather, PDP-8\tab programming and\par
\tab binary loading support is considered an independent concept.\par
  \tab This is especially disappointing for use on the PDP-12 where\par
\tab mixed dual mode assembly is quite common due to the nature of the\par
\tab architecture.\par
\par
End of Appendix B\page\f0\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f1\par
Appendix C - P?S/8 PAL conditional literals and related topics\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 PAL supports an extension beyond the literal features \tab common to other assemblers as described earlier in this document, \tab which is known as the \i conditional\i0  literal.\par
\par
\tab Note: An alternative term for the conditional literal is the \tab\i dependent\i0  literal since the actual code generated is dependent \tab upon specific conditions in effect throughout the assembly.\par
\par
  \tab While the use of conditional literals is an important and \tab powerful feature of practical PDP-8 assembly language \tab programming, conditional literal support is unique to P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
  \tab Note: Future directions of P?S/8 support include the P?S/8 SHELL \tab overlay which will include a new assembler program partially \tab based on P?S/8 PAL; conditional literal support will be \tab consistent with P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
Alternate syntax considerations of conditional literals.\par
\par
\tab To aid in transitioning from other assemblers (such as Macro-11) \tab to PDP-8 programming, the conditional literal uses syntax similar \tab to that of certain other assemblers:\par
\par
\f0\tab\tab TAD\tab\tab #10\tab\tab\tab /ADD 0010 FROM SOMEWHERE.\par
\par
\f1\tab While addressing rules for the PDP-8 differ from other computer\tab architectures, literals expressed this way are a familiar concept \tab to many programmers accustomed to the software environment of \tab these other systems; the use of conditional literals in P?S/8 PAL\par
\tab will help programmers make the transition to the PDP-8 assembly\par
\tab language environment easier.  See Note 1.\par
\par
Conditional literal syntax and addressing rules.\par
\par
\tab Conditional literals take the form of the following:\par
\par
\f0\tab\tab TAD\tab\tab #3\tab\tab\tab /ADD 0003 FROM CHOSEN PAGE.\par
\f1\par
\tab In this example, the assembler will make a dynamic decision as to \tab which type of literal to use as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) If the program already contains a matching Page Zero literal\par
\tab    due to other program statements using the identical value,\par
\tab    this statement will use that existing Page Zero literal\par
\tab    address.\par
\par
\tab b) Assuming a) fails, If the program already contains a matching\par
\tab    current page literal value due to other program statements,\par
\tab    the statement will use that current page literal address.\page\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab c) Assuming both a) and b) fail, the assembler will create a new \tab    current page literal; the new literal address is used.\par
\par
\tab Note: It is conceivable current page literal statements on the \tab same page could benefit by using the same literal address should \tab the current page literal statement occur later in the assembly.\par
\par
\tab Unlike Page Zero and current page literals, there is no matching \tab optional trailing character.  As such, the seldom-needed feature \tab of nested expression elements is not available when using \tab conditional literals.  See Note 1.\par
\par
\tab In certain cases, it may be necessary to calculate factors of an\par
\tab overall equation to be used with some form of literal statement.\par
\tab Each such factor would be carried out by an equate statement\par
\tab leading to the final expression used in the literal statement.\par
\tab In the case of conditional literals this is unavoidable due to\par
\tab lack of a closing expression character.  See Notes 2 and 3.\par
\par
Source libraries and conditional literals.\par
\par
\tab Experienced programmers tend to create a series of commonly used \tab routines as starting points for a new programming project.  \tab Unlike other architectures (some constrained by ROM-based basic\par
 \tab routines), the PDP-8 imposes no particular structure, other than\par
\tab a general convention that the last page of field 0 (07600-07777)\par
\tab is reserved for whatever mechanism is used to load the program.\par
\par
\tab Note: Depending on the particular operating system and certain \par
\tab specific configuration considerations, there may be additional \tab limitations imposed; however, the emphasis is always on minimal \tab intrusion regarding memory reservation.  P?S/8 contains specific\par
\tab information within the kernel memory in 07600-07777 to allow a\par
\tab conforming program to determine what (if any) additional\par
\tab limitations are in effect; other operating systems may implement\par
\tab additional constraints on memory usage.\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 adds basic read/write routines to designated storage \tab device(s) that are available by the nature of P?S/8 internal \tab design.  By proper adherence to system guidelines, programs can\par
\tab be made compatible with all supported machine models including \tab the \i DECmate\i0  series (which is substantially incompatible with all \tab previous 12-bit DEC computers).\par
\par
\tab As stated above, by conforming to P?S/8 system guidelines, any\par
\tab program can run under P?S/8 on any supported model; however,\par
\tab there is no hard and fast rule that this is required.  Any\par
\tab program can be written for any specific supported model(s)\par
\tab constrained only by the designated hardware (and the minimal \tab memory reservation rules of P?S/8, which must be observed to\par
\tab allow P?S/8 to load the executable form of the program into \tab memory).\page\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\par
\par
\cf0\tab For practical program execution, foundational programming is\par
\tab needed to support many of the specific structures mentioned\par
\tab above; many elements of this support will be needed nearly all\par
\tab the time while others might be needed less often.  In the general\par
\tab case, it is advantageous to develop working modules that can be\par
\tab organized into source libraries for each class of functions\par
\tab desired.  See Note 4.\par
\par
\tab Well-formed source library routines for use in the P?S/8 \tab environment should exclusively use conditional literals wherever \tab possible; existing Page Zero literals defined in the main \tab program will be used to determine \tab the specific binary code\par
\tab generated.  As such, the code size of the library routines will \tab tend to be shorter without expending the effort to determine this \par
\tab manually (as would be the case when using other assemblers\par
\tab lacking this feature unique to P?S/8 PAL).  See Note 5.\par
\par
Problematic interaction with incompatible assemblers regarding conditional literals.\par
\par
\tab Attempts to maintain compatibility with other assemblers can be\par
\tab frustrating when a programming project requires \tab the conditional\par
\tab literal feature to avoid excessive development time.  Using P?S/8\par
\tab PAL (and conditional literals), a \tab nearly finished project can\par
\tab produce a contemporaneous assembly listing which can then be\par
\tab studied to determine instances that were successfully assembled\par
\tab using existing Page Zero literals.  This is a good starting point\par
\tab to create compatible source code.  However, any further changes\par
\tab to the source code can easily invalidate the inter-symbol\par
\tab relationships that led to the optimization that existed only\par
 \tab before making further source code modifications.\par
\par
\tab Any work performed at this point leads only to a tenuous form of \tab compatibility; changes in the coding may not translate into an\par
\tab efficient program, just merely a program that had been efficient\par
\tab at the time the necessary compatibility statements were written.\par
\tab The result is hardly a good outcome, but is the only way to\par
\tab achieve some measure of compatibility at the expense of efficient\par
\tab design, achieved with minimal effort to this point, then modified\par
\tab with additional effort that only partially supports the tentative\par
\tab compatibility once further changes are made.\par
\par
Implementation of compatible coding.\tab\par
\par
\tab Conditional assembly techniques can be used to determine which \tab assembler is processing the source code and choose alternate\par
\tab source code statements where necessary.  While this tedious\par
\tab process produces the same binary at the time the effort\par
\tab is spent determining which statements to code both for P?S/8 PAL\par
\tab and also other assemblers (such as PAL8), the alternate\par
\tab statements cannot dynamically adjust to the changing requirements\par
\tab of an ongoing project when future modifications are made.\page\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab This is not by any means a completely viable alternative and \tab should only be implemented very close to the end of any project.  \tab Those responsible for program maintenance need to be aware of \tab potential consequences of future coding changes.\par
\par
\tab Note: This is (in part) why certain programmers familiar with\par
\tab P?S/8 PAL abandon PAL8 for some portion of their work.\par
\par
\tab The following example illustrates how to write code that \tab assembles in a custom manner with P?S/8 PAL:\par
\f0\par
\tab\tab IFNDEF\tab PQS\tab <PQS= 0> \tab /THIS IS IGNORED BY P?S/8 PAL.\f1\par
\par
\tab\tab IFZERO\tab PQS\tab <\tab\tab /ASSEMBLE FOR THE OTHERS.>\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNZRO\tab PQS\tab <\tab\tab /ASSEMBLE FOR P?S/8 PAL ONLY.>\par
\par
\tab Various techniques can be implemented for each instance of a \tab known successful conditional literal; the goal is to produce\par
\tab binary code that is identical regardless of which assembler is\par
\tab used (unless and until the overall source code optimization is\par
\tab updated).  The following serves as a minimal guideline for\par
 \tab constructing compatible dual statement sections:\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNZRO\tab PQS\tab <\tab\tab /ASSEMBLE FOR P?S/8 PAL ONLY.\par
\par
     \tab AND\tab\tab #77\tab\tab\tab /JUST SIX BITS.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab >\par
\par
     \tab IFZERO\tab PQS\tab <\tab\tab /ASSEMBLE FOR THE OTHERS.\par
\par
     \tab AND\tab\tab [77]\tab\tab\tab /JUST SIX BITS.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab >\par
\par
\tab All statements similar to the above, where the conditional \tab literal successfully used a Page Zero literal value, must be\par
\tab coded as shown in the example.\par
\par
\tab Additional techniques, such as surrounding the conditionalized\par
\tab code with judiciously chosen XLIST directive statements can\par
\tab achieve what appears to be unaffected code if desired:\par
\par
\tab\tab XLIST\tab OFF\tab\tab\tab /GENERALLY DON'T WANT TO SEE THIS.\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNDEF\tab OFF\tab <OFF= 1>  /THIS TURNS LISTING OFF.\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNDEF\tab ON\tab <ON=\tab 0>\tab /THIS TURNS LISTING ON.\par
\par
\tab\tab XLIST\tab ON\tab\tab\tab /WHAT FOLLOWS WILL BE SEEN.\page\tab\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab These parameters are used to debug the conditional sections.  If \tab unexpected output occurs, the definition of \i OFF\i0  can be \tab temporarily changed to reveal all conditional sections (at the \tab expense of paging of the code in a listing file) to aid in \tab determining what was miscoded.\par
\par
\tab Note: The definitions of the controlling variables (OFF and ON\i )\i0  \tab are unimportant during pass 1 of the assembly as long as they are \tab eventually defined before the end of the source code.\par
\par
\tab All dual-coded sections should start with \ul XLIST OFF\ulnone .  Each \tab section starts with an \ul XLIST ON\ulnone  and ends with \ul XLIST OFF\ulnone .  \tab Following the entire conditional section, \ul XLIST ON\ulnone  should be\par
\tab used.\par
\par
\tab Note: All statement lines with an instance of XLIST are not\par
\tab listed.  However, P?S/8 PAL (and PAL10) assembly listings will\par
\tab reveal gaps in the statement numbers.  Listings created with PAL8\par
\tab and CREF have incremental line numbers since there is no ability\par
 \tab to detect the consequences of XLIST statements.\par
\par
\tab The example below implements all of the compatibility features \tab discussed above:\par
\par
     \tab XLIST\tab OFF\tab\tab\tab /START OF CONDITIONAL SECTION.\par
\par
     \tab IFNZRO\tab PQS\tab <\par
\par
     \tab XLIST\tab ON\par
     \tab AND\tab\tab #77\tab\tab\tab /JUST SIX BITS.\par
     \tab XLIST\tab OFF\tab >\par
\par
     \tab IFZERO\tab PQS\tab <\par
\par
     \tab XLIST\tab ON\par
     \tab AND\tab\tab [77]\tab\tab\tab /JUST SIX BITS.\par
     \tab XLIST\tab OFF\tab >\par
\par
     \tab XLIST\tab ON\tab\tab\tab /END OF THIS CONDITIONAL SECTION.\line\line\tab Note: Every aspect of this technique must be repeated for each\par
\tab instance of a conditional literal statement successfully using a\par
\tab Page Zero literal value.  When assembled by PAL8/CREF the code\par
\tab appears to be innocuous; however, the actual program logic and\par
\tab effort to implement is completely obfuscated.\par
\par
More advanced techniques.\par
\par
\tab If the programmer chooses to spend additional effort regarding\par
\tab these issues, statements can be formed to trace conditional \tab literal statements that succeed in producing Page Zero usage.\page\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\par
\cf0\par
  \tab The following statement is to be used only once early in the \tab source code before any conditional literal considerations:\par
\par
\tab\tab ZCNT=\tab 0\tab\tab\tab /INITIALIZE SUCCESS COUNT.\par
\par
\tab Within each conditional section being traced, additional \tab statements are added just after the code statement using the \tab conditional literal as follows:\par
\par
     \tab IFNZRO\tab TRC\tab\tab <\tab /ASSEMBLED IF THE TRACE IS ENABLED.\par
\par
     \tab ZUSED=\tab #77\tab\tab\tab /WILL BE ADDRESS THAT WAS USED.\par
\par
     \tab IFZERO\tab ZUSED&200\tab <\par
\par
     \tab ZCNT=\tab ZCNT+1\tab\tab /COUNT THIS AS A SUCCESS.\par
\par
     \tab PAUSE\tab ZCNT\tab\tab\tab /OUTPUT CURRENT COUNT.\par
\par
     \tab PAUSE\tab .\tab\tab\tab /SHOW WHERE WE ARE\par
\par
          \tab\tab\tab\tab >\par
\par
     \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab >\tab /END OF TRACE FOR THIS SECTION.\par
\par
\tab This additional coding is intended for all conditional literal \tab statements without regard to the likelihood of Page Zero literal\par
\tab value outcome.  The tracing statements are designed to reveal the\par
\tab statements that succeeded; this allows monitoring the \tab effectiveness of conditional literal usage.\par
\par
\tab The PAUSE directive used with an evaluated argument is a feature\par
\tab exclusive to P?S/8 PAL.  Each usage of the PAUSE directive \tab outputs a message to the prevailing listing output device; the \tab printout includes the four digit octal evaluation of the \tab arithmetic expression following the PAUSE directive as well as \tab the current address within the assembly.  As used in the example,\par
 \tab the count of successful instances appears to \i grow\i0  with each new\par
\tab successful case; the address within the assembly will aid in\par
\tab adding the compatibility code changes required as shown above.\par
\par
\tab Note: PAUSE output is not an error message.\par
\par
Real-world application example.\par
\par
\tab A large programming project that was materially affected by the\par
\tab use of conditional literals is shown below.  Statements were\par
\tab added for backward compatibility (with PAL8) as described above:\par
\par
\tab A large PDP-8/E system was created for a corporate setting to run \tab several online systems (and an offline backup system that could \tab share data).\page\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\par
\cf0\par
 \tab This project is one of the most ambitious programs ever written\par
\tab for the 32K PDP-8 environment.  It was written as a standalone\par
\tab bootable operating system with external system generation or \tab creation routines; the storage consisted of a pair of RK05 drives\par
\tab on an RK8E with optional third-party serial-line multiplexors.\par
\tab This system is capable of running as many as 40 tasks in a\par
\tab cooperative multitasking environment.\par
\par
\tab Due to the sheer complexity (which includes overlays of certain \tab maintenance and data recovery tools) this was perhaps the best \tab known example of productivity improvement achieved by the use \tab of conditional literals.  Had the feature not been available, \tab development time would have profoundly increased.\par
\par
\tab However, an unfortunate postscript to this otherwise successful \tab example includes the following:\par
\par
\tab The small company division that contracted for this system was \tab also occasionally managed by individuals from the parent company.  \tab These individuals were not only totally unfamiliar with the local\par
\tab division operation, but also totally ignorant of all things\par
\tab PDP-8.  As a direct result of their so-called \i executive\i0  actions,\par
\tab the company paid for many additional weeks of needless make-work\par
\tab to implement PAL8 compatibility as described earlier.\par
\par
  \tab They were warned at the time that future modifications would \tab likely result in increased development costs and time.  This is \tab exactly what happened during the course of several years as the \tab requirements gradually changed.  All of the necessary conditional\par
\tab sections were consistently implemented as described above; the \tab cost predictably increased due to the additional effort to \tab reconcile the code as the project gradually changed direction.\par
\par
\tab Since the entirety of this large application is completely \tab standalone by nature, such decisions were quite foolhardy.\par
\par
\tab It is interesting to note the parent company was eventually \tab forced to sell the local division to another large company, and \tab in fact, eventually was forced into bankruptcy.  The new owners \tab had \tab notions of their own which did not include the use of any \tab DEC products; they decided to dismantle the equipment and then \tab specified a replacement system that would cost several million \tab dollars.  However, since they also seriously devalued the entire \tab local company operation, they were forced to sell it to an even \tab larger company that eventually replaced everything with specific\tab overpriced PC compatible systems that could have easily been\par
\tab produced for far less overall cost.\par
\page\tab\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\cf0\par
\par
\tab As of the writing of this document, the corporate world perceives\tab this operation as requiring tens of millions of dollars worth of \tab hardware despite company growth of less than 20% in the over \tab twenty years of operation the author of this document is familiar \tab with; this era ended with the head of the local operation seeking \tab early retirement due to all of the incompetent interference over \tab the years.\par
\par
Alternative methods to achieve some measure of compatibility.\par
\par
\tab The use of conditional literals is a useful technique for many\par
\tab PDP-8 programming projects.  If there is a need to produce a\par
\tab result that can be used in OS/8, there are appropriate techniques\par
\tab available to allow P?S/8 PAL to be used for program development.\par
\par
\tab a) P?S/8 generally runs on any configuration that OS/8 can\par
\tab    support (including configurations OS/8 cannot run on due to\par
        its design limitations regarding system device handler\par
\tab    design).\par
\par
\tab b) P?S/8 \i OS8CON\i0  is a bidirectional text file conversion program\par
\tab    designed to facilitate moving PAL (and other language) source\par
\tab    files between the two systems (in either direction of\par
\tab    conversion).  While each configuration is different, it is\par
\tab    generally possible to create a compatible transfer mechanism\par
 \tab    on most (if not all) of the devices supported by both systems.\par
\par
\tab    With minimal effort, it is possible to maintain files in OS/8\par
\tab    and also transfer copies of the files to P?S/8 to allow\par
 \tab    processing with P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
\tab c) Depending on project complexity, there are currently several\par
\tab    provisional methods to transfer assembled binary code between\par
\tab    P?S/8 and OS/8.  This involves taking advantage of fortuitous\par
\tab    coincidences between the two systems with regard to field 0\par
\tab    usage coupled with the fact that P?S/8 can boot to OS/8 while\par
\tab    preserving the contents of extended memory across the reboot\par
\tab    process.\par
\par
\tab d) For reasons unrelated to OS/8 binary compatibility as\par
 \tab    discussed within this document, a near-term future component\par
\tab    of P?S/8 is being developed known as \i BINCON\i0 .  BINCON will be\par
\tab    capable of converting OS/8 absolute binary files to/from P?S/8\par
\tab    \f0 Slurp\i\f1  \i0 format binary files.  This will allow P?S/8 source\par
\tab    development to not only continue to be written conforming to\par
\tab    the subset of PAL8 compatibility (where feasible), but will\par
\tab    also allow a misimplemented feature of PAL8 (\i DTORG\i0  which was\par
\tab    originally implemented for the benefit of the Typeset-8 group,\par
\tab    which corrupts the binary output for all other OS/8-related\par
\tab    purposes) to become useful for P?S/8 development in the near\par
\tab    future.\page\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab    Certain P?S/8 source programs are intended to be assembled and \tab    loaded in the OS/8 environment; this produces a stand-alone\par
\tab    system generation technique for a portion of current P?S/8\par
\tab    system development.  BINCON will allow changes to this process\par
\tab    while also maintaining compatibility with the current method.\par
\tab    P?S/8 PAL will produce proper P?S/8 binary files which can\par
\tab    then be converted to OS/8 format.  Programmers can use\par
\tab    analogous techniques to take advantage of conditional\par
\tab    literals (and perhaps other features such as dual-mode\par
\tab    assembly), yet maintain source files (and converted binary\par
\tab    files) in OS/8.\par
\par
\tab e) P?S/8 and OS/8 can be run from the P?S Enhanced PDP-8\par
\tab    Simulator (PEPS) package for Windows (which is available from\par
\tab    the author of this document).  Due to the way the package\par
\tab    is designed, it is possible to punch binary output in DEC BIN\par
\tab    format within one system and then read it back into the other\par
\tab    system, etc.\line\line\tab    As such, P?S/8 PAL can create an \ul\i\f0 assemble-and-punch\ulnone\i0  operation\par
\tab    using a single command with the appropriate command-line\par
\tab    option switches to produce binary output to the high-speed\par
\tab    punch.  Exiting the simulator back to the Windows command\par
\tab    level, the high-speed punch data file can be reassigned as the\par
\tab    high-speed reader data.  The simulator can then be run booting\par
\tab    OS/8.  OS/8 PIP can be used to create an image binary copy of\par
\tab    the high-speed reader input using the PTR: handler.\f1\page\cf2 Appendix C Notes\par
\par
\cf0 1)\tab The syntax of the conditional literal was proposed as introduced \tab by the \highlight3\\\highlight0  character.  As of P?S/8 PAL Version 8P, the \highlight3 #\highlight0  character\par
\tab is used.  A cosmetic advantage of the original proposal was to \par
\tab allow pseudo-symmetrical statements to be formed:\par
\par
\tab TAD     \\3/\tab\tab /ADD 0003 FROM DYNAMICALLY CHOSEN PAGE.\par
\par
\tab No actual symmetry exists; in the example above, the line ends \tab with a comment starting just after the end of the conditional \tab literal with the first \highlight3 /\highlight0  character; to the casual eye, the \tab comment starts with the second \highlight3 /\highlight0  character (in the usual place \tab further to the right).\par
\par
\tab This syntax was abandoned when dual-mode assembly was added to\par
\tab P?S/8 PAL.  LINC mode code requires the \highlight3\\\highlight0  character be used for\par
\tab left-shift 9 operations    associated with LINCtape programming.\par
\par
2)\tab PAL8 has several bugs in its arithmetic expression handling \tab that render it incompatible with P?S/8 PAL and PAL10, which\par
\tab largely implement most expression features compatibly; however,\par
\tab the weaknesses of PAL8 are avoidable for most (but not all) PDP-8\par
 \tab programming projects.\par
\par
\tab Note: Areas of weakness and incompatibility are the focus of this\par
\tab section; the goal is to guide all users by providing useful\par
\tab workarounds that can be deployed in all relevant operating\par
\tab systems.\par
\par
\tab In general, P?S/8 program development features maintain \tab compatibility \tab with the PAL8 implementation \tab as released with\par
\tab\ul\i OS/278 Version 2\ulnone\i0 .  Certain bugs exist in earlier versions of\par
\tab PAL8 which were never fixed; however, these are generally\par
\tab esoteric in nature and for the most part easily avoided.  In a\par
\tab few specific instances, the exact syntax in statements within\par
\tab P?S/8 source code files was slightly compromised to maintain\par
\tab compatibility with PAL8; however, this is admittedly a relatively\par
\tab minor issue.  As described above, both PAL10 and PAL8 lack\par
\tab conditional literal support, which is a key conceptual feature\par
\tab that is crucial to realistic large-scale program development.\cf3\par
\cf0\tab\par
\tab A large PDP-8 project was written attempting to overcome this \tab serious issue using conditional assembly techniques to manage\par
\tab the incompatibility (with no ability to fully overcome the\par
\tab problem).  During development, many assembly listings were\par
\tab analyzed to edit conditional assembly statements to best serve\par
\tab the project.  Since the project managers demanded that PAL8 be\par
\tab used, this was a less than satisfying outcome wasting much time\par
\tab and effort.\cf2\par
\par
\cf0\tab Note: In hindsight, the programmers should have fought management\par
\tab more aggressively to prove their decisions were detrimental to\par
\tab the company's bottom line.\page\cf2 Appendix C Notes (continued)\par
\cf0\par
3)\tab The use of parenthetical expressions in the context of literals \tab is an intended design goal.  Unfortunately, PAL8 parenthetical\par
\tab expression support is poorly implemented and has many unexpected\par
\tab limitations and odd quirks.  It is generally recommended that \tab such expressions be avoided unless the only assemblers used are\par
 \tab PAL10 or P?S/8 PAL; both of these assemblers properly implement\par
\tab Page Zero and current page literals regardless of complexity.\par
\par
\tab By design and where applicable, conditional literals must be\par
\tab calculated using separate equate statements; however, the use of \tab parenthetical expressions is an obscure feature of the PAL \tab language which is seldom employed.  Unfortunately, this led to \tab failure to test poor implementations such as PAL8.  With regard\par
\tab to arithmetic expressions in general, PAL8 has several blunders\tab\tab besides literal expressions, such as a flawed implementation of\par
\tab unary expressions.\par
\tab\par
\tab Note: When few programmers use obscure features, bugs tend to go \tab unnoticed and are less likely to be fixed.\par
\par
4)\tab Due to the versatility of the PDP-8 instruction set (especially\par
\tab the \i JMS\i0  instruction) there are often multiple ways to create \tab callable subroutines that, while similar in function, are not\par
\tab identical in form or usage.  At the whim of the programmer, \tab preferred variations can be placed into a source library, each\par
\tab with different tradeoffs; as a project is developed, choices are \tab made as to which routines are best to use.\par
\par
\tab For example, a subroutine may be defined for the purpose of \tab passing a text string meant to be displayed on the system \tab console.  Every aspect of the mechanism of the subroutine is \tab subject to change.  A subroutine entry point may be located near\par
\tab the end of Page Zero intended to be addressed directly by each\par
\tab caller using \ul\i JMS 0177\ulnone\i0  or similar.  Alternatively, a pointer may\par
\tab be placed on Page Zero to allow callers scattered throughout\par
\tab memory equal access to the subroutine; multiple callers on\par
 \tab several memory pages would justify the use of a Page Zero\par
\tab pointer.  By using conditional literals, a proper dynamic\par
\tab decision can be made with regard to appropriate generation of\par
\tab subroutine linkage pointers.\par
\par
\tab An implementation detail might specify the use of information \tab passed in the accumulator, such as an upper limit on message\par
\tab print length (in case the message could exceed some restricted\par
\tab amount).  Alternately, the accumulator could contain flag bits\par
\tab indicating what action to take afterwards (such as displaying\par
\tab\highlight3 <CR>\highlight0  and \highlight3 <LF>\highlight0 ).  Other possibilities could include the\par
\tab disposition of specially crafted text message strings capable of\par
\tab mixed upper-case and lower-case display.\page\cf2 Appendix C Notes (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab The calling sequence could dictate the string exists inline past \tab the subroutine call; the subroutine should return to just past \tab the word containing the string terminator character.  \tab\par
\par
\tab Alternatively, the address of the string could be passed inline \tab and the subroutine returns to the location following the inline \tab pointer.  If the scope of the subroutine includes extended \tab memory support, additional bits may be passed in a variety of\par
\tab ways to form a full 15-bit address of where the string is located\par
\tab within the potential 32K memory space.\par
\par
\tab Not every variation is needed in every situation and all \tab decisions are subject to preference.  As such, source libraries\par
\tab are not only highly specific to a programmer's needs, there are\par
\tab many ways to carry out common functions and a sophisticated\par
\tab programmer may expend effort developing elegant mechanisms to \tab exploit in future projects.\par
\par
\tab Note: Some features may inherently be somewhat inefficient of\par
\tab memory utilization in exchange for functionality; experienced\par
\tab programmers know what is the most suitable for any given \par
\tab situation.  With experience, additional projects may mandate \tab additional methods to expand programming techniques.\par
\par
5)\tab Note: Assuming several uses of a common value within library \tab sections, as long as one usage anywhere in the program is \tab\i hardwired\i0  as a Page Zero literal, all related instances will \tab also effectively become Page Zero literals.  In some cases, it \tab may be desirable to include comments about likely combinations \tab within the relevant library routines.\par
\par
  \tab For example, two independent library routines may both use a \tab common literal value; if only one routine is referenced in the \tab main program, the use of actual Page Zero literal references \tab would depend on the usage of a hardwired Page Zero literal in the \tab main code.  If both sections are included in the program, \tab changing any usage to a hardwired Page Zero literal would \tab ensure all other instances would become Page Zero references \tab without regard for the main program code.  Conditional assembly \tab techniques can be applied to elegantly manage situations of this \tab nature.\par
\par
\tab Assume subroutine #1 contains the first (potentially Page Zero) \tab conditional literal while subroutine #2 contains a second \tab occurrence of the same (potentially Page Zero) conditional \tab literal.\par
\par
 \tab The programmer could create a mechanism to account for \tab subroutines used in the library such as the following:\page\cf2 Appendix C Notes (continued)\par
\cf0\par
        SUB1=   1        /SUBROUTINE #1 IS USED IN THIS PROGRAM.\par
\par
        SUB2=   1        /SUBROUTINE #2 IS USED IN THIS PROGRAM.\par
\par
\tab The body of code in the library for subroutine #1 would be \tab bracketed within the following statements.\par
\par
        IFNZRO  SUB1    <\par
\par
        /       THIS IS WHERE THE ACTUAL SUBROUTINE #1 CODE GOES.\par
\par
                        >\par
\par
\tab Similarly for subroutine #2:\par
\par
        IFNZRO  SUB2    <\par
\par
        /       THIS IS WHERE THE ACTUAL SUBROUTINE #2 CODE GOES.\par
\par
                        >\par
\par
\tab For the actual conditional literal section in question, \tab management statements similar to the following can implement the \tab example described above:\par
\par
\tab (This is the section where the potential Page Zero conditional \tab literal is located within subroutine #1.)\par
\par
        IFZERO  SUB2    <\par
\par
        AND     #77             /REMOVE UPPER SIX BITS.\par
\par
                        >\par
\par
        IFNZRO  SUB2    <\par
\par
        AND     [77]           /REMOVE UPPER SIX BITS.\par
\par
                        >\cf2\par
\cf0\par
\tab The second subroutine uses the conditional literal form of a \tab similar statement; the actual binary code generated within \tab subroutine #2 is a Page Zero literal if a) the main program uses \tab the same Page Zero literal, or b) subroutine #1 uses the explicit \tab Page Zero reference because it was aware of the identical usage \tab within subroutine 2.\page\cf2 Appendix C Notes (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Future releases of P?S/8 PAL may make mechanisms such as this \tab slightly easier to implement.  There exists an obscure assembler \tab for PDP-8 code including literal support and support for the \tab instructions of the FPP-12 (and FPP-8/A, E).  It was designed and \tab implemented by Jack Burness for use with \i DIAL-MS\i0  (the operating \tab system he wrote exclusively for the 8K PDP-12 with one of a small \tab class of specific disk storage devices).\par
\par
\tab This assembler is known as \i FPPASM\i0  and differs from the embedded \tab DIAL assembler in the following particulars:\par
\par
\tab a) The DIAL assembler supports LINC mode assembly, FPPASM does\par
\tab    not.  (P?S/8 PAL supports LINC mode assembly as a modular\par
\tab    option which can be enabled by the use of command-line option\par
\tab    switches.)\par
\par
\tab b) The DIAL assembler does not support literals of any form \par
\tab    while FPPASM supports Page Zero and current page literals in a\par
\tab    manner generally compatible with PAL10, PAL8 and P?S/8 PAL.\line\par
\tab c) The DIAL-MS assembler and the FPPASM assembler support certain\par
\tab    directives and conditional assembly mechanisms incompatible\par
\tab    with PAL10, PAL8 and P?S/8 PAL (when dual assembly mode is\par
\tab    disabled).  All of the directives unrelated to conditional\par
\tab    assembly represent alternative syntax for the functionality\par
\tab    supported by the three assemblers as stated above; in some\par
\tab    cases, these features are less powerful and represent older\par
\tab    language elements generally abandoned in favor of the newer\par
\tab    directives.  Any program can become fully compatible among the\par
\tab    three assemblers by incorporating appropriate substitutions.\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 PAL supports most of the FPPASM conditional directives if \tab the proper command-line switches are used.\par
\par
\tab Additional directives available in FPPASM may be implemented in \tab future releases of P?S/8 PAL which are helpful when supporting \tab source code libraries.\par
\par
\tab The exact syntax of these directives will be made more compliant\par
\tab with the other standard PDP-8 assembler directives; other \tab directives used within FPPASM have already been added to P?S/8\par
\tab PAL for full compatibility with the DIAL assembler.\cf2\par
