Glossary of Terms\par
\par
 1.\tab BIN format\par
\par
\tab The standard binary loading format in the DEC \ul\i Paper-tape\par
\ulnone      \ul operating system\ulnone\i0  is known as DEC \i BIN\i0  format which is optimized\par
\tab for loading data from paper-tape frames.  This format includes\par
\tab both leader and trailer frame codes to delineate sets of data.\par
 \tab The highest tape channel is used as a visual aid to help position\par
\tab paper-tapes in the reader; all leader and trailer frames have \tab only the high-order bit punched.  Data frames may or may not have\par
\tab this bit punched, but clearly have some combination of the other \tab bits also punched.  All leader frames are ignored. \tab Loading\par
\tab ends when the first trailer frame is encountered.  DEC BIN format\par
 \tab incorporates all necessary elements needed to load 12-bit\par
\tab words into memory including a 12-bit checksum at the end of the\par
\tab tape; memory field change frames are included as required.\par
\par
\tab Note: field change frames have the two highest bits punched. \tab Additionally, field change frames are not included in the overall\par
 \tab tape checksum.  It is surmised this came about because the format\par
\tab was upgraded when machines larger than 4K memory were first\par
\tab produced, this could have had compatibility issues with DEC BIN \tab format tapes produced somewhat earlier.\line\line\tab DEC BIN format is inherently compromised in terms of loading\par
\tab efficiency because of the need to support leader and checksum\par
\tab codes that are superfluous in an operating system environment\par
\tab (such as P?S/8).  Storage devices inherently have device parity\par
\tab checking for all data; leader and trailer frame codes serve no\par
\tab purpose when data is stored in blocks on a mass \tab storage device.\par
\par
\tab While certain systems use an adaptation of DEC BIN format for\par
\tab binary files, P?S/8 uses the more versatile and efficient Slurp\i\par
\tab\i0 binary loading format described elsewhere; Slurp format is\par
\tab optimized for efficient loading.  The P?S/8 binary utility\par
\tab program (also known as \i BIN\i0 ) includes utilities to convert between\par
\tab paper-tape data and binary file data (in both conversion\par
\tab directions).\par
\par
\tab Note: Throughout this document, BIN refers to the P?S/8 BIN\par
 \tab utility as mentioned above.  More information about P?S/8 BIN can\par
\tab be obtained from other documents such as the \ul\i P?S/8 System \par
\ulnone\i0\tab\ul\i Programs Guide\ulnone\i0  available separately.\page\cf2 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0  \par
\par
 2.\tab Edit buffer\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 includes a set of embedded commands in the keyboard monitor \tab which allow editing of a text file directly (without loading a \tab separate editing program).  This design concept allows for rapid \tab editing of portions of a larger project that generally extends \tab across several TFS files.  A file can be created from scratch or\par
\tab an existing file can be loaded into the edit buffer within the\par
 \tab keyboard monitor.  Files are tentative until the edit buffer\par
\tab contents are saved to a named file in the TFS directory of the \tab system device (or one of the other supported logical device \tab units).\line\line\tab While there are some tradeoffs when compared to more traditional \tab file editing methods, generally there is only incidental need for \tab additional storage space; most files are updated in the edit \tab buffer and then written back to the same file slot on the system \tab device.  Certain users also create backup copies of important \tab files to ensure the preservation of the file under edit, but this \tab practice is not mandatory.\par
\par
\tab Note: All files of a project may be saved to an alternate logical \tab unit with the same (or variant) file names.  Backing up project \tab files (periodically) in this manner generally makes the creation \tab of individual backup files moot.  Many users do not bother with\par
\tab any form of file backup.\par
\par
 3.\tab Extended-length text files\par
\par
\tab Extended-length files partially resemble TFS text files except no\tab line number or line pointer data exists within the file.\par
\tab Additionally, there are no length restrictions on extended-length\par
\tab files (other than the applicable limits of the storage device).\par
\par
 4.\tab Logical Console Overlay\par
\par
\tab The \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone  is an optional component of P?S/8.\par
\tab When enabled, all conforming programs will direct all system\tab console (and lineprinter) input/output to subroutine calls to \tab designated  memory locations instead of the usual physical\par
\tab devices.  While the use of the Logical Console Overlay requires\par
\tab the availability of extended memory, certain advanced features\par
\tab become available as described below:\cf2\par
\cf0\par
\tab a) If the P?S/8 system device handler detects an error, the usual\tab    action is to halt the CPU (often displaying device-dependent\par
\tab    status bits in the accumulator); pressing \highlight3 CONTINUE\highlight0  on the\par
\tab    system front panel will safely retry the latest read/write\par
\tab    operation.  Users need to be familiar with the particular\par
\tab    system device handler in use to gain insight as to what is\par
 \tab    causing the specific problem by examining the contents of the\par
\tab    accumulator (if feasible).\page\cf2 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab    Note: A system option is available to disable the system halt,\par
\tab    resulting in an infinite retry after any error; depending on\par
\tab    hardware particulars, this setting is generally not\par
\tab    recommended.  (Ironically, using this setting causes error\par
\tab    handling to resemble the mediocre error recovery techniques\par
\tab    usually implemented in many OS/8 device handlers.)\par
\par
\tab    When the \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone  is enabled, the default\par
\tab    action taken by the system device handler is transformed into\par
\tab    issuing a status report on the system console device (which is\par
\tab    potentially redefined by the \ul Logical Console Overlay\i\par
\ulnone\tab    \i0 configuration to be directed to alternative hardware).  This\par
\tab    generally includes details of the latest read/write attempt.\par
\tab    The user can choose which action to take (at the point of\par
\tab    error) by entering one of the options appearing on the system\par
\tab    console keyboard.  This allows the user to correct the\par
\tab    problem (if possible) and retry the latest read/write\par
\tab    operation, or to abort the latest operation (if necessary).\par
 \tab    Even without having specific knowledge regarding the vagaries\par
\tab    of the current system device, any user can easily manage all\par
\tab    system device handler recovery operations (perhaps after\par
\tab    consulting some device-specific documentation).\cf2\par
\cf0\par
\tab b) Not every system device handler is capable of optimal\par
 \tab    performance; this is especially true of system device handlers\par
        designed to run on 4K systems (without requiring loading of a\par
        portion of the handler into extended memory).  Due to the\par
\tab    limited memory space on 4K systems, it is often necessary to\par
\tab    compromise performance to obtain basic functionality.\par
\par
        When the \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone  is enabled, additional\par
        functionality (designed into the system device handler for\par
\tab    this purpose) is also enabled.  This generally implements\par
\tab    additional performance improvements that should overcome the\par
 \tab    original design compromises needed to obtain basic\par
\tab    functionality.\cf2\par
\cf0\par
        Note: On hardware configurations where the system device\par
\tab    handler requires an extended-memory segment, the \ul Logical\ulnone\par
\tab    \ul Console Overlay\ulnone  is designed to load into lower memory\par
\tab    locations within the same (highest) memory field.  As such,\par
\tab    for conforming programs (such as P?S/8 PAL) attempting to\par
\tab    locate extended memory fields, the presence of the \ul Logical\ulnone\par
\tab    \ul Console Overlay\ulnone  does not change the count of fully available\par
\tab    memory fields.  In certain circumstances, programs may take\par
\tab    advantage of the 3/4 memory field available if the \ul Logical\i\par
\ulnone\tab    \ul\i0 Console Overlay\ulnone  is disabled.  For example, P?S/8 PAL may\par
\tab    support up to 768 additional symbols if this memory space is\par
\tab    available.\page\cf2 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab c) Certain configurations of the \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone  may use\par
 \tab    alternate console hardware (such as a console terminal with\par
\tab    interface device codes differing from 03/04).  In certain\par
\tab    configurations (based on terminal emulators), an error panel\par
\tab    screen may temporarily replace the normal display of console\par
\tab    output until the error is resolved.\par
\par
 5.\tab RIM format\par
\par
\tab While BIN format (as described elsewhere in this document) is the\par
\tab standard format for loading most binary paper-tape programs, \i RIM\par
\i0\tab format is generally used to first load the BIN loader.  While RIM \tab format is more inefficient, this is generally not an issue when\par
\tab loading relatively short programs.\cf2\par
\par
\cf0\tab Some users want a minimal overall solution to loading certain \tab particular programs; as such, the \tab binary data might be punched\par
\tab in RIM format, eliminating the need for the BIN loader as an\par
\tab intermediate loading program.\par
\par
\tab RIM format paper-tapes use the same overall frame layout as that\par
\tab used with the BIN loader; the differences are as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) Auto-update of the memory loading pointer is not supported.\par
\tab    Each loaded word must include an origin setting preceding the\par
\tab    data word.\par
\par
\tab b) There is no support for an overall checksum in a RIM binary\par
\tab    paper tape.\par
\par
\tab    Note: There are various techniques available to include an\par
\pard\li720    optional checksum at the end of a RIM format binary paper\par
\pard\tab    tape; this adds the ability for the BIN loader to load the\par
\tab    same program as an alternative to the RIM loader.\par
\par
\tab c) No support for Field settings.  The user must set the loading\par
\tab    field from the front panel Data Field switches or equivalent\par
\tab    means; this setting applies throughout the entire RIM loading\par
\tab    session.\par
\par
\tab d) Two equally short versions of the DEC standard RIM loader\par
        exist.  One supports the high-speed reader; the other supports\par
\tab    the low-speed reader of the model ASR 33 (or ASR 35) Teletype\par
\tab    often used as the system console device.  All versions of the\par
\tab    BIN loader are far longer programs; either of the RIM loader\par
\tab    variants can be used to quickly load the BIN loader; as such,\par
\tab    the RIM\i  \i0 loader is often hand-toggled into memory using the\par
\tab    computer's front panel switches.\page\cf2 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
 6.\tab Slurp binary file format and Slurp binary file loader\par
\par
\tab\i Slurp \i0 is the name given to the P?S/8 binary file format by its\par
\tab inventor, Richard Lary.  (The name is suggestive of the mechanism\par
\tab by which the loader functions.)  Mr. Lary and his colleague Lenny \tab Elekman are the principal authors of the \ul\i R-L Monitor System\ulnone\i0 .\par
\tab P?S/8 is (partially) based on this earlier system.  Both\par
\tab operating systems support Slurp format binary files as created by\par
\tab P?S/8 PAL and other utilities; due to implementation restrictions\par
\tab within the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone , there is no support for extended\par
\tab memory loading.\cf2\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: The assembler used in the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  is the DEC\par
\tab\ul Paper-Tape Operating System\ulnone  assembler known as \ul\i PAL III\ulnone\i0 .  Certain \tab modifications were made to write binary output to the system\par
\tab device in Slurp binary format.  Although binary output is limited\par
\tab to designated system blocks, additional commands exist to \par
\tab transfer the binary output to user-designated Slurp format binary\par
\tab files in the TFS directory (when and where feasible, which is not\par
\tab always the case due to fixed file length considerations).  P?S/8\par
\tab PAL supports Slurp format binary output files directly, which\par
\tab eliminates this problem; the default binary output design was\par
\tab remedied as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) The block layout of the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  is such that Slurp\par
\tab    format binary output is always written starting at absolute\par
\tab    block 0022.  There is a fixed file that can be generally\par
\tab    referenced (as if it were in the TFS directory) located at\par
\tab    blocks 0040-0057; in all command scenarios, this is accessed\par
\tab    using the \ul\i $\ulnone  \i0 symbol.  If sufficiently long Slurp binary output\par
\tab    is created, it can easily overlap the storage space of the \ul $\par
\ulnone\tab    file; as long as the length is less than 0020 blocks (meaning\par
\tab    that no more than blocks 0040-0041 have been overwritten\par
\tab    within the \ul $\ulnone  file), the Slurp format binary data can be\par
\tab    transferred to a named file in the TFS directory.  However,\par
\tab    some programs create larger Slurp format binary data (which\par
\tab    had been allowed to be as long as blocks 0022 through 0057\par
\tab    without damage to the operating system).  Such programs had\par
\tab    to be reassembled and then loaded from the default Slurp\par
\tab    binary area should there had been any activity that might\par
\tab    change these specific circumstances (such as writing over the\par
\tab    \ul $\ulnone  file).\line\line         Due to an implementation restriction of the R-L Monitor BIN\i\par
\i0  \tab    utility, it is impossible to load the contents of the actual \ul $\ulnone\par
\tab    file; the syntax used to access it instead refers to the Slurp\par
\tab    format binary output area starting in block 0022.  (The BIN\par
\tab    program reinterprets the starting block of the first input\par
\tab    file as 0022 if the actual passed value is 0040.)\page\cf2 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab    Note: R-L Monitor system program names are limited to two\par
\tab    characters, thus R-L Monitor \i RU\{n\}\i0  commands typically\par
\tab    reference \i PA\{L\}\i0  and \i BI\{N\}\i0 , etc.  Additional characters are\par
\tab    allowed on the command line, but will be ignored.\par
\line\tab b) P?S/8 changes certain aspects of the operating system block\par
\tab    layout such that blocks 0020-0037 can be referenced with the\par
\tab    symbol \ul\i %\ulnone\i0  which is analogous to the \ul $\ulnone  file (which references\par
\tab    blocks 0040-0057) in all relevant command contexts.\cf2\par
\cf0\par
\tab    The storage blocks occupied by the two files (\ul %\ulnone  and \ul $)\ulnone  are\par
\tab    also accessed as the virtual image of field 0 once created by\par
\tab    the \i GET\i0  command (or equivalent).  In this context, direct\par
\tab    usage of the two file names should be avoided to prevent\par
\tab    destruction of the core image; however, the image contents can\par
\tab    be saved (and restored at a later time if desired) by using a\par
\tab    series of keyboard monitor FE\{tch\} and WR\{ite\} commands as\par
\tab    required.  If there is no requirement to preserve the core\par
\tab    image, the two files can be used independently in lieu of TFS\par
\tab    files (which are generally faster to access due to close\par
\tab    proximity to the beginning of the storage device).\par
\par
\tab    Note: while the P?S/8 SHELL overlay will (generally) not\par
\tab    reference the TFS file structure, the field 0 virtual area is\par
\tab    identically defined as the same blocks used in the basic P?S/8\par
\tab    system.  This can be useful when changing between the basic\par
\tab    P?S/8 and the P?S/8 SHELL for further program development and \tab    related logistic issues.\par
\line\tab    P?S/8 PAL includes the /B, /D and /U command-line option\par
\tab    switches to access these files without having to provide\par
\tab    explicit output file references (where applicable).\line\line\tab    Thus, any reference to the \ul %\ulnone  file (whether explicitly as a\par
\tab    stated output file, or by use of the /B command-line option\par
\tab    switch passed to PAL) is the closest equivalent to the\par
\tab    original functionality in the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  (without the\par
\tab    potential file destruction problem).\par
\par
\tab    Note: The basic P?S/8 execution-class commands access system\par
\tab    programs with up to six character names.  For programs with\par
\tab    exactly six characters in the name, the commands can be\par
\tab    embellished using additional characters of the user's choosing\par
\tab    (all of which are ignored).  As such, this is not an issue for\par
\tab    relevant programs as discussed here including PAL, BIN, GET,\par
 \tab    START, ODT (since all of them have shorter program names).\page\cf2 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab The fastest loading of binary data into randomly accessed memory\par
\tab locations is achieved using the Slurp binary format method.\par
\tab Actual loading efficiency approaches 6/7 of block-oriented (core-\par
\tab image) binary format while allowing the loading of arbitrary data\par
\tab ranging from individual memory locations through large blocks of\par
\tab contiguous memory without requiring any form of block structure.\par
\tab Memory can be freely loaded anywhere in the entire PDP-8 32K\par
\tab memory space (other than system reserved memory areas).\par
\par
\tab Note: All implementations of the Slurp loader prior to P?S/8 were\par
\tab limited to loading into field 0 only; P?S/8 uses an extension to\par
\tab the format that doesn't impact on 4K usage.  An appropriate\par
\tab\i CDF XX\i0  instruction to the new memory loading field is required\par
\tab when the loading field changes.\cf2\par
\cf0\par
\tab Since the P?S/8 Slurp binary file loader is a superset of the\par
\tab original 4K-only versions as used is several other PDP-8 systems,\par
\tab files transferred from the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  can be loaded\par
\tab without internal conversion.\par
\par
\tab Since P?S/8 PAL supports chaining to P?S/8 BIN, all loading\par
\tab options of the Slurp binary file loader are available during a\par
\tab chain operation; additional details are described elsewhere in\par
\tab this document.\par
\par
\tab A list of other operating systems that support a Slurp format \tab binary loader follows:\par
\par
\tab a) \ul The R-L Monitor System\ulnone  (also known as \i MS/8\i0  as submitted to\par
